"""A Level Biology (Lower Sixth): Biodiversity & Systematic Taxonomy; Autotrophic & Heterotrophic Nutrition."""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("biology", "lower-sixth")

TAX = "Biodiversity & Systematic Taxonomy"
NUT = "Autotrophic & Heterotrophic Nutrition"

LESSONS = []

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Domains and kingdoms
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=TAX,
    topic="Domains and kingdoms",
    title="Classifying life: domains, kingdoms and the binomial system",
    slug="bil2-domains-and-kingdoms",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Place any organism in the taxonomic hierarchy, name it with a binomial, and explain the three-domain and five-kingdom schemes and the evidence behind them.",
    objectives=[
        "List the taxonomic ranks from domain to species and use the binomial system correctly",
        "Explain why species are hard to define and state the biological species concept",
        "Distinguish the three domains using cell and molecular features",
        "Assign organisms to the five kingdoms from their features",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Taxonomy", "Taxon", "Binomial nomenclature", "Species", "Domain", "Five kingdoms", "Archaea", "Phylogeny"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"In a market in Douala you can buy plantains, mangoes, fish, snails, mushrooms and palm wine. Every seller has a local name for each item, and the names change from one language to the next. Biologists need one name that every scientist in the world understands. **Taxonomy** is the science of naming and grouping living things. **Classification** puts organisms into groups based on their similarities and, today, on their evolutionary relationships."),
        H("The taxonomic hierarchy"),
        D(r"A **taxon** (plural **taxa**) is any group in a classification, at any rank. Each rank contains one or more taxa of the rank below it. This is a **hierarchy**: groups nested inside bigger groups, with no overlap between groups at the same rank.", title="Taxon and hierarchy"),
        CODE("Rank      Example: human        Example: oil palm\nDomain    Eukarya               Eukarya\nKingdom   Animalia              Plantae\nPhylum    Chordata              Angiospermophyta (flowering plants)\nClass     Mammalia              Monocotyledonae\nOrder     Primates              Arecales\nFamily    Hominidae             Arecaceae\nGenus     Homo                  Elaeis\nSpecies   Homo sapiens          Elaeis guineensis", "text", title="Table 1. Classification of two organisms"),
        T(r"A memory aid for the ranks is *Dear King Philip Came Over For Good Soup* (Domain, Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species). Going down the list, groups get smaller and members share more features. Some books use the word *division* for a plant phylum, and the exact names of plant classes differ between books: **check your textbook**."),
        H("The binomial system"),
        D(r"The **binomial system** (devised by Carl Linnaeus) gives every species a two-part Latin name. The first part is the **genus** (capital first letter); the second is the **species** name (all lower case). The name is printed in italics, or underlined when handwritten.", title="Binomial nomenclature", note="Example: Theobroma cacao (the cocoa tree, grown widely in Cameroon's Centre and South-West regions)."),
        T(r"Why Latin? It is a neutral language that no country owns, and the names do not change when languages change. The genus name may be shortened after first use (*T. cacao*), but never the species name alone. Two species in the same genus, such as *Elaeis guineensis* (African oil palm) and *Elaeis oleifera*, are close relatives."),
        MISTAKE("Writing the name wrongly", r"Marks are lost for *homo Sapiens*, *Homo sapiens* typed in plain letters, or for the species name alone (*sapiens*). Write: genus with a capital, species in lower case, the whole name underlined or in italics."),
        H("What is a species?"),
        D(r"In the **biological species concept**, a species is a group of organisms that can interbreed in nature to produce **fertile** offspring, and that are reproductively isolated from other such groups.", title="Species"),
        T(r"A horse and a donkey can mate, but their offspring, the mule, is sterile. So horses and donkeys are different species. The concept has limits: it cannot be applied to organisms that reproduce only asexually (most bacteria), or to fossils, and some different species do sometimes produce fertile hybrids. For these cases biologists also compare anatomy, DNA sequences and ecology. Textbooks give more than one species concept: **check your textbook** for the ones your syllabus uses."),
        H("Three domains"),
        T(r"In the 1970s Carl Woese compared the base sequences of ribosomal RNA (rRNA), a molecule found in every cell, and showed that prokaryotes are not one group. The kinds of cell are now placed in three **domains**, the highest taxonomic rank."),
        KEY("The three domains", "Bacteria and Archaea are prokaryotes with no nucleus; Eukarya have a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles.", cards=[
            ["Bacteria", "Prokaryotes. Cell wall of peptidoglycan. Membrane lipids with ester bonds. Circular DNA not wrapped around histones. Includes Escherichia coli and cyanobacteria."],
            ["Archaea", "Prokaryotes. No peptidoglycan in the wall. Membrane lipids with ether bonds. DNA associated with histone-like proteins. Many live in extreme habitats such as hot springs and salt lakes, but not only there."],
            ["Eukarya", "Eukaryotes. DNA in a nucleus with true histones. Membrane-bound organelles and 80S ribosomes. Includes protoctists, fungi, plants and animals."],
        ]),
        T(r"Archaea look like bacteria under the microscope, but their RNA polymerase, the way their genes are expressed and their rRNA resemble those of eukaryotes more than those of bacteria. This molecular evidence is why they are placed in a separate domain."),
        H("Five kingdoms"),
        T(r"Within the domains, organisms are placed in kingdoms. Many A Level courses use the five-kingdom scheme of R. H. Whittaker, where the two prokaryote domains are together called Prokaryotae (also called Monera). Other books use six or more kingdoms, or split Protoctista into several groups: **check your textbook** for the scheme your course follows."),
        IMG("al-bil2-classification-tree.svg", alt="Tree: life divides into the domains Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya; Eukarya divides into the kingdoms Protoctista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia; Bacteria and Archaea together make the kingdom Prokaryotae in the five-kingdom scheme.", caption="Figure 1. The three domains and the five kingdoms. Prokaryotae (dashed) covers both prokaryote domains."),
        CODE("Kingdom       Cell type     Nutrition                  Cell wall            Examples\nProkaryotae  prokaryote    auto- or heterotrophic     peptidoglycan*       E. coli, cyanobacteria\nProtoctista  eukaryote     auto- or heterotrophic     varies               Amoeba, Chlorella, kelp\nFungi        eukaryote     heterotrophic (absorb)     chitin               Rhizopus, yeast, mushroom\nPlantae      eukaryote     autotrophic (photosyn.)    cellulose            moss, fern, maize\nAnimalia     eukaryote     heterotrophic (ingest)     none                 earthworm, tilapia, human\n* Bacteria only; Archaea lack peptidoglycan", "text", title="Table 2. Features of the five kingdoms"),
        TIP(r"Viruses are not in any kingdom or domain. They have no cells, no metabolism of their own, and reproduce only inside a host cell, so most biologists do not regard them as living organisms."),
        EX("Worked example 1: classify a species", intro=r"Write the full classification of the African grey parrot, *Psittacus erithacus*, a bird traded in the forests of southern Cameroon. It is a eukaryote that ingests food, has no cell wall, a backbone, feathers and a beak.", steps=[
            S("Decide the domain from cell type", text="It has a nucleus, so the domain is Eukarya."),
            S("Decide the kingdom from nutrition and cell wall", text="It ingests food and its cells have no wall: Animalia."),
            S("Use the backbone for the phylum and the feathers for the class", text="Phylum Chordata; class Aves (birds)."),
            S("Add the lower ranks given by the name", text="Genus *Psittacus*; species *Psittacus erithacus*."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: choosing the kingdom", intro=r"A specimen has cells with a nucleus and cell walls made of chitin. It grows as thin threads and feeds by secreting enzymes onto bread and absorbing the digested food. Name its kingdom.", steps=[
            S("Cell type", text="Nucleus present: a eukaryote, so not Prokaryotae."),
            S("Cell wall", text="Chitin walls point to Fungi (plants have cellulose; animals have no wall)."),
            S("Nutrition", text="Extracellular digestion then absorption is typical of Fungi."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The kingdom is Fungi."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: choosing the domain", intro=r"A microorganism from a hot spring has no nucleus. Its cell wall contains no peptidoglycan and its membrane lipids have ether bonds. Name its domain.", steps=[
            S("Nucleus", text="No nucleus: Bacteria or Archaea."),
            S("Wall and lipids", text="No peptidoglycan and ether-linked lipids are features of Archaea, not Bacteria."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Domain Archaea."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("Which pair of organisms is most likely to be in the same genus?", ["Elaeis guineensis and Elaeis oleifera", "Elaeis guineensis and Theobroma cacao", "Homo sapiens and Pan troglodytes", "Equus caballus and Bos taurus"], 0,
             "Species in the same genus share the first part of the binomial name.",
             hint="Look at the first word of each name.",
             feedback=["", "The genus names differ (Elaeis and Theobroma), so they are not in the same genus.", "The genus names differ (Homo and Pan).", "The genus names differ (Equus and Bos)."]),
        QUIZ("Which feature separates Archaea from Bacteria?", ["Archaea have a nucleus", "Archaea have no peptidoglycan in their cell walls", "Archaea have 80S ribosomes", "Archaea have no DNA"], 1,
             "Bacterial walls contain peptidoglycan; archaeal walls do not, and archaeal membrane lipids have ether bonds.",
             hint="Both groups are prokaryotes, so think about wall chemistry.",
             feedback=["Archaea are prokaryotes with no nucleus.", "", "Archaea have 70S ribosomes, like Bacteria.", "All cells have DNA."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"State **two** reasons why a horse and a donkey are classed as different species even though they can mate.", r"They produce a mule, which is sterile, so they do not form one interbreeding population producing fertile offspring. They are normally reproductively isolated in nature and have different numbers of chromosomes (horse 64, donkey 62), which causes problems in forming gametes in the hybrid."),
        PQ(r"Explain why classifying organisms using rRNA base sequences is more reliable than using appearance alone.", r"rRNA is present in all cells and changes slowly, so sequences can be compared across all organisms. Differences in sequence reflect how long ago two groups shared an ancestor, so the grouping shows evolutionary relationships. Appearance can mislead because unrelated organisms can look alike through convergent evolution."),
        PQ(r"Give **three** differences between the domains Bacteria and Eukarya.", r"Bacteria have no nucleus (DNA free in the cytoplasm), Eukarya have DNA in a nucleus. Bacteria have 70S ribosomes, Eukarya have 80S in the cytoplasm. Bacteria have no membrane-bound organelles, Eukarya do. Bacterial walls contain peptidoglycan; eukaryote walls (where present) are cellulose or chitin. Bacterial DNA is circular and not wound around histones."),
        PQ(r"Write the binomial name for a species of genus *Musa* with species name *paradisiaca*, and say how it should be written in an examination.", r"*Musa paradisiaca*: the genus *Musa* has a capital M, the species name *paradisiaca* is in lower case, and the whole name is underlined (or in italics). (Banana and plantain names vary between books: check your textbook.)"),
        SUM(
            "Taxonomic ranks run Domain, Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species; groups are nested with no overlap.",
            "A binomial is genus (capital) plus species (lower case), in italics or underlined.",
            "Biological species concept: interbreed to give fertile offspring; it fails for asexual organisms and fossils.",
            "Three domains (Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya) are based on rRNA and cell chemistry; Bacteria and Archaea are prokaryotes.",
            "The five kingdoms are Prokaryotae, Protoctista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia; check your textbook for the scheme your course uses.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2. Dichotomous keys
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=TAX,
    topic="Dichotomous keys",
    title="Using and building dichotomous keys",
    slug="bil2-dichotomous-keys",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Identify organisms with a numbered dichotomous key, write good paired statements, and construct a key for a small set of specimens.",
    objectives=[
        "Follow a numbered dichotomous key to identify a specimen",
        "Write paired statements that use clear, observable features",
        "Construct a key to separate a given set of organisms",
        "Explain the difference between a key and a natural classification",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Dichotomous key", "Couplet", "Observable feature", "Identification", "Branching key"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A student on a field trip near Mount Cameroon collects six small animals under logs. To name them, she does not need to memorise every species. She uses a **dichotomous key**: a series of paired questions that leads step by step to a name. *Dichotomous* means 'divided into two'."),
        D(r"A **dichotomous key** is a set of numbered **couplets**. Each couplet has two contrasting statements about an observable feature. Each statement either gives the name of the organism or sends you to another numbered couplet.", title="Dichotomous key"),
        T(r"A key is a tool for **identification**. It is not a classification: the order of questions is chosen to separate the specimens quickly, not to show evolutionary relationships. Two animals may end up close together in a key and still be distantly related."),
        H("Using a key"),
        CODE("1a  Legs present ............................. go to 2\n1b  Legs absent ............................. go to 5\n2a  More than eight legs ...................... go to 3\n2b  Eight legs or fewer ....................... go to 4\n3a  Two pairs of legs on most segments ........ millipede\n3b  One pair of legs on each segment .......... centipede\n4a  Six legs ................................. housefly\n4b  Eight legs ............................... spider\n5a  Body inside a coiled shell ............... snail\n5b  No shell, body made of many rings ........ earthworm", "text", title="Key 1. Six small animals (a teaching key written for this lesson)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: using the key", intro=r"A specimen has no legs, no shell, and a soft body made of many rings. Use Key 1 to name it.", steps=[
            S("Couplet 1", text="No legs matches 1b, so go to 5."),
            S("Couplet 5", text="No shell and a ringed body matches 5b."),
            S("Result", text="The specimen is an earthworm."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a longer path", intro=r"A specimen has ten legs on each side of its long body, one pair on each body segment. Use Key 1.", steps=[
            S("Couplet 1", text="Legs are present: 1a, go to 2."),
            S("Couplet 2", text="More than eight legs: 2a, go to 3."),
            S("Couplet 3", text="One pair on each segment: 3b."),
            S("Result", text="The specimen is a centipede."),
        ]),
        TIP(r"Read **both** statements of a couplet before choosing, even when the first one seems to fit. If neither statement fits, go back: you probably made a mistake earlier, or the organism is not in the key."),
        H("Writing good couplets"),
        KEY("Rules for a good key", "A key must work for someone who has never seen the organism.", cards=[
            ["Observable", "Use features that can be seen with the eye or a hand lens: leg number, wings, shell, leaf shape."],
            ["Contrasting", "The two statements must be opposites. If an organism matches one, it cannot match the other."],
            ["Not variable", "Avoid colour, size or age; they vary within a species. Say 'more than eight legs', not 'large'."],
            ["Complete", "Every specimen must end on a name, and every couplet must have exactly two choices."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Using size, colour or comparisons", r"A statement such as 'large leaves' or 'brown body' fails because young and old specimens differ and different people judge 'large' differently. Use counts, presence or absence, or clear shapes."),
        H("Building a key"),
        T(r"To build a key for a set of specimens, make a table of their features, then pick a feature that **splits the set into two groups**. Repeat for each group until every specimen is alone."),
        CODE("Plant      Leaf type    Veins      Margin\nMaize      simple       parallel   entire\nCassava    compound     net        entire\nMango      simple       net        entire\nHibiscus   simple       net        toothed", "text", title="Table 1. Four leaves (invented teaching data)"),
        EX("Worked example 3: construct a key", intro=r"Build a dichotomous key for the four leaves in Table 1.", steps=[
            S("Find a feature that isolates one plant at once", text="Only cassava has compound leaves. Couplet 1: compound leaf, cassava; simple leaf, go to 2."),
            S("Split the remaining three", text="Only maize has parallel veins. Couplet 2: parallel veins, maize; net veins, go to 3."),
            S("Split the last two", text="Margin entire gives mango; margin toothed gives hibiscus."),
            S("Write the key", text="1a Leaf compound: cassava. 1b Leaf simple: go to 2. 2a Veins parallel: maize. 2b Veins net: go to 3. 3a Margin entire: mango. 3b Margin toothed: hibiscus."),
        ]),
        T(r"Notice that four organisms need three couplets: a key for $n$ organisms needs $n - 1$ couplets when every couplet splits into two. If your key has more, some couplets are wasted; if fewer, some organisms are missing."),
        KEY("Other forms of key", "The same information can be shown in other ways.", cards=[
            ["Numbered (as above)", "Written as couplets that send you to other numbers. Compact and easy to print."],
            ["Branching (flow chart)", "Each question splits into two arrows, drawn as a tree. Easy to follow, but takes more space."],
            ["Computer or card key", "Several features are chosen at once and the matches are filtered. Used in field guides and apps."],
        ]),
        QUIZ("A key contains the statement: 'Plant is tall.' Why is this a poor statement?", ["Because it uses a colour", "Because height varies with age and conditions and is judged differently by different people", "Because keys must use only Latin words", "Because it has only one statement"], 1,
             "A good key statement is observable and does not vary. 'Tall' is a comparison with no limit.",
             hint="Think of a young plant and an old plant of the same species.",
             feedback=["Height is not a colour.", "", "Keys use ordinary language.", "The problem is the vague feature, not the number of statements."]),
        QUIZ("A key is made to separate seven species. How many couplets does a complete dichotomous key need?", ["3", "6", "7", "8"], 1,
             "Each couplet separates one group into two, so n organisms need n - 1 couplets: 6.",
             hint="Four organisms needed three couplets in the example.",
             feedback=["Three couplets can separate at most four organisms.", "", "That is the number of organisms, not couplets.", "That is one more than the number of organisms."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"Use Key 1 to identify a specimen with six legs. State the couplets you used.", r"Legs present: 1a, go to 2. Eight legs or fewer: 2b, go to 4. Six legs: 4a. The specimen is a housefly. (Couplets used: 1, 2 and 4.)"),
        PQ(r"A student writes this couplet: '1a Animal is small. 1b Animal is large.' Give **two** faults and rewrite it.", r"Faults: size is a relative comparison and varies with age, so people will not agree; and neither statement describes a real structure. Rewrite using a countable feature, e.g. 1a Body longer than 10 cm; 1b Body 10 cm or shorter (measured with a ruler), or better use 1a Wings present; 1b Wings absent."),
        PQ(r"Construct a key for these three fruits: pawpaw (many small black seeds, soft flesh), mango (one large stone, soft flesh), groundnut (hard dry pod, several seeds inside).", r"1a Fruit has a hard dry case: groundnut. 1b Fruit has soft flesh: go to 2. 2a One large seed (stone): mango. 2b Many small seeds: pawpaw. (Three fruits need two couplets.)"),
        PQ(r"Explain why a dichotomous key is not the same as a classification.", r"A key is built to identify specimens quickly, using any easily seen feature; the order of the questions has no evolutionary meaning. A classification groups organisms by shared ancestry and many shared features, and the groups (taxa) are meant to show relationships."),
        SUM(
            "A dichotomous key is a series of numbered couplets, each with two contrasting statements.",
            "Always read both statements and follow the number or name given.",
            "Use observable, constant features: counts, presence or absence, shapes; never size, colour or age.",
            "To build a key, split the set into two groups by one feature, then repeat; n organisms need n - 1 couplets.",
            "A key identifies organisms; it does not show evolutionary relationships.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. Protoctista and Fungi
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=TAX,
    topic="Protoctista and Fungi",
    title="Protoctista and Fungi: features and life cycles",
    slug="bil2-protoctista-and-fungi",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Describe the diversity of Protoctista, the life cycle of Plasmodium, the structure and nutrition of fungi, and the life cycle of Rhizopus, comparing fungi with plants.",
    objectives=[
        "State the features of Protoctista and give examples of protozoa and algae",
        "Describe the life cycle of Plasmodium in the mosquito and the human",
        "Describe the structure and feeding of a fungus and the life cycle of Rhizopus",
        "Compare fungi with plants and with animals",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Protoctista", "Protozoa", "Algae", "Plasmodium", "Hypha", "Mycelium", "Chitin", "Saprotroph", "Sporangium", "Zygospore"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Malaria is caused by a single-celled organism, *Plasmodium*, and still kills many children in Cameroon. Bread left in a damp kitchen grows a white fuzz that later turns black: a fungus, *Rhizopus*. Both belong to eukaryote groups that students find hard to classify, because neither is a plant nor an animal. This lesson describes these two kingdoms and one life cycle from each."),
        H("Kingdom Protoctista"),
        D(r"**Protoctista** is the kingdom of eukaryotes that are not plants, animals or fungi. Most are unicellular, some form colonies or simple multicellular bodies, and they have no true tissues organised into organs.", title="Protoctista", note="This kingdom is a 'leftover' group. Modern books divide it into several lines of descent: check your textbook."),
        KEY("Three common kinds", "The kingdom is sorted informally by how its members feed.", cards=[
            ["Protozoa (animal-like)", "Heterotrophs that ingest food. Amoeba moves by pseudopodia; Paramecium swims with cilia; Plasmodium is a parasite."],
            ["Algae (plant-like)", "Photosynthetic, with chloroplasts. Chlorella and Spirogyra (green algae) and kelp (brown algae). Cell walls usually contain cellulose."],
            ["Slime and water moulds", "Fungus-like feeders. Phytophthora (an oomycete) causes blight of potato and also attacks cocoa pods in the humid cocoa areas."],
        ]),
        TIP(r"Do not say 'algae are plants'. Algae have no roots, stems, leaves or vascular tissue, and do not protect an embryo, so they belong to Protoctista."),
        H("Plasmodium and malaria"),
        T(r"*Plasmodium* is a protoctist parasite with two hosts. The female *Anopheles* mosquito is the **vector** (carries the parasite from person to person) and humans are the host in which asexual reproduction takes place. *Plasmodium falciparum* is the species causing the most severe malaria in Cameroon."),
        CODE("1  Infected mosquito bites a person: sporozoites enter the blood.\n2  Sporozoites invade liver cells and divide: merozoites released.\n3  Merozoites invade red blood cells and feed on haemoglobin.\n4  Each parasite divides (schizont) and bursts the cell:\n   new merozoites released together with toxins -> fever.\n5  Cycle 3-4 repeats every 48 h (P. falciparum varies) -> repeated fevers.\n6  Some merozoites become gametocytes in the blood.\n7  A mosquito sucks blood: gametocytes fuse in its gut\n   -> zygote -> oocyst in gut wall -> sporozoites\n   -> move to salivary glands, ready for the next bite.", "text", title="Life cycle of Plasmodium"),
        T(r"Fever comes in waves because many parasites burst out of red blood cells at about the same time. The sexual stage (fertilisation) is in the mosquito, so the mosquito is the **definitive host** in many textbooks; some books use the opposite words, so **check your textbook**. Control targets the mosquito (bed nets, removing stagnant water) and the parasite (antimalarial drugs)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: explain the pattern of fever", intro=r"A patient has fever every second day. Explain why, using the life cycle.", steps=[
            S("Name the stage that causes fever", text="Merozoites divide inside red blood cells, and the infected cells burst, releasing merozoites and toxins into the blood."),
            S("Explain the timing", text="The parasites in the blood develop at the same rate, so most red cells burst at the same time, about every 48 hours."),
            S("Link to the cycle", text="After bursting, the merozoites quickly invade new red cells and the cycle starts again, so the fever repeats."),
        ]),
        H("Kingdom Fungi"),
        D(r"**Fungi** are eukaryotes whose body is made of threads called **hyphae**. The mass of hyphae is the **mycelium**. Cell walls contain **chitin**. Fungi are heterotrophs: most are **saprotrophs** that secrete digestive enzymes onto dead organic matter and absorb the soluble products.", title="Fungi"),
        T(r"This feeding is called **extracellular digestion** followed by **absorption**. The mycelium has a large surface area for absorption. Fungi store carbohydrate as **glycogen**, as animals do, not starch. They reproduce by spores that are carried by air, and they have no motile stage."),
        CODE("Feature              Fungi                       Plants                      Animals\nCell wall            chitin                      cellulose                   none\nNutrition            absorb (heterotroph)        photosynthesis              ingest (heterotroph)\nStorage              glycogen                    starch                      glycogen\nBody                 hyphae / mycelium           roots, stems, leaves        organs and tissues\nChloroplasts         absent                      present                     absent", "text", title="Table 1. Comparing fungi with plants and animals"),
        MISTAKE("Calling hyphae cells or roots", r"A hypha is a thread, often with many nuclei and no cross-walls (as in *Rhizopus*), or divided by cross-walls called septa (as in most mushrooms and moulds). Fungi have no roots; the mycelium is not a plant organ."),
        H("Rhizopus: a mould on bread"),
        T(r"*Rhizopus* grows on bread, yams and fruit. Its hyphae spread over the surface, with **rhizoids** that anchor and absorb. Upright hyphae called **sporangiophores** carry black round **sporangia** in which haploid spores are made by mitosis. The spores are light, and are blown on to new food. This is **asexual reproduction** and it is fast."),
        T(r"Sexual reproduction occurs when two hyphae of different mating types (called + and −) grow towards each other and fuse. Their nuclei fuse to make a diploid **zygospore** with a thick black wall that can survive drought. The zygospore later undergoes meiosis and germinates to produce a sporangium, whose spores are genetically varied."),
        CODE("Haploid mycelium (+) ----\n                               >-- fusion of hyphae --> zygospore (2n, thick wall)\nHaploid mycelium (-) ----                                  |\n                                                          meiosis, germination\n                                                            |\n   sporangium releases haploid spores <-- germ sporangium (n)\n   (spores also made by mitosis in ordinary sporangia)", "text", title="Sexual cycle of Rhizopus"),
        EX("Worked example 2: asexual versus sexual", intro=r"Explain why *Rhizopus* uses both asexual and sexual reproduction.", steps=[
            S("Asexual spores", text="Many spores are made quickly by mitosis, so the fungus spreads rapidly while food is plentiful. The spores are genetically identical."),
            S("Sexual zygospore", text="Fusion of different mating types shuffles genes (meiosis makes new combinations), and the thick-walled zygospore survives harsh conditions."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Asexual reproduction gives speed in good conditions; sexual reproduction gives variation and survival in bad conditions."),
        ]),
        H("Yeast and other fungi"),
        T(r"Yeast (*Saccharomyces*) is a unicellular fungus. It reproduces by **budding**: a small cell forms on the parent and breaks away. In low oxygen yeast ferments glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide, which is used in baking and in the fermentation of palm sap to palm wine. Mushrooms are fungi whose fruiting body is a mass of tightly packed hyphae that carries spores. Some fungi live on plant roots as **mycorrhizae**, helping the plant to absorb water and phosphate while receiving sugars. Fungi are placed in phyla according to their sexual structures (for example Zygomycota, Ascomycota, Basidiomycota); the naming differs between books, so **check your textbook**."),
        EX("Worked example 3: identify the kingdom", intro=r"An organism grows as threads with chitin walls on a dead log. It has no chloroplasts and stores glycogen. Explain why it is not a plant.", steps=[
            S("Cell wall", text="Plant walls are cellulose, but this wall is chitin."),
            S("Nutrition", text="It has no chloroplasts, so it cannot photosynthesise; it absorbs food from the log."),
            S("Storage", text="Plants store starch, but this organism stores glycogen."),
            S("Conclusion", text="All three features show a fungus."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("Which stage of the Plasmodium life cycle is injected into a person by a mosquito?", ["Merozoite", "Gametocyte", "Sporozoite", "Zygospore"], 2,
             "Sporozoites collect in the mosquito's salivary glands and enter the blood with its saliva.",
             hint="Think of the stage found in the salivary glands.",
             feedback=["Merozoites are released from liver cells and red blood cells inside the human.", "Gametocytes are taken up by the mosquito when it feeds.", "", "A zygospore belongs to Rhizopus, a fungus."]),
        QUIZ("What is the main storage carbohydrate of fungi?", ["Starch", "Cellulose", "Glycogen", "Chitin"], 2,
             "Fungi store glycogen, like animals.",
             hint="Which storage carbohydrate do fungi share with animals?",
             feedback=["Starch is the storage carbohydrate of plants and algae.", "Cellulose is a plant wall material, not a store.", "", "Chitin forms the wall; it is not a store of energy."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"Describe **three** features that distinguish fungi from plants.", r"Fungal walls are made of chitin (plants: cellulose). Fungi have no chloroplasts and are heterotrophs that digest food externally and absorb it (plants photosynthesise). Fungi store glycogen (plants store starch). Fungal body is of hyphae forming a mycelium, not roots, stems and leaves."),
        PQ(r"Explain why *Plasmodium* cannot be classified in the kingdom Fungi.", r"Plasmodium has no chitin wall and no hyphae; it is a single-celled parasite that lives inside cells and shows no mycelial body. It does not absorb food by extracellular digestion but feeds on the haemoglobin of red cells. It is placed in Protoctista."),
        PQ(r"Describe how a person is infected with *Plasmodium* and how the parasite then multiplies in the human body.", r"An infected female Anopheles mosquito bites and injects sporozoites. These enter the liver, where they divide and release merozoites. Merozoites enter red blood cells and divide again until the cells burst, releasing more merozoites, which infect new red cells."),
        PQ(r"Explain **two** advantages to *Rhizopus* of producing spores.", r"Spores are small and light, so they are dispersed widely by air and can reach new food. Large numbers are produced quickly, so the colony spreads rapidly and some will land on suitable food. (Zygospores and spores can also survive dry conditions.)"),
        SUM(
            "Protoctista are eukaryotes that are not plants, animals or fungi: protozoa, algae and mould-like groups.",
            "Plasmodium: sporozoites into the liver, merozoites into red blood cells, bursting causes fever, gametocytes taken by Anopheles, fertilisation in the mosquito.",
            "Fungi have chitin walls, hyphae forming a mycelium, extracellular digestion with absorption, and glycogen stores.",
            "Rhizopus reproduces asexually by sporangiospores and sexually by a diploid zygospore formed by fusion of + and − hyphae.",
            "Naming of fungal phyla and the protoctist groups varies; check your textbook.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4. Plantae 1: bryophytes and pteridophytes
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=TAX,
    topic="Plantae",
    title="Plantae I: alternation of generations in mosses and ferns",
    slug="bil2-plantae-bryophytes-ferns",
    minutes=24,
    summary="State the features of the plant kingdom, explain alternation of generations, and compare the life cycles and structure of bryophytes (mosses) and pteridophytes (ferns).",
    objectives=[
        "State the features shared by all plants",
        "Explain alternation of generations and where meiosis and mitosis occur",
        "Describe the structure and life cycle of a moss and a fern",
        "Compare bryophytes with pteridophytes in structure, vascular tissue and dependence on water",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Alternation of generations", "Sporophyte", "Gametophyte", "Bryophyta", "Pteridophyta", "Vascular tissue", "Sorus", "Prothallus"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"On wet rocks beside a waterfall you find a green cushion of moss. In the shade of a forest in the Bamenda highlands you find ferns with long, divided leaves. Both are plants, yet they are very different from a maize plant. They show the first steps by which plants moved from water on to land, and they all share one unusual feature: a life cycle with two different bodies."),
        H("What makes a plant?"),
        D(r"**Plantae** are multicellular eukaryotes with cell walls of cellulose and chloroplasts containing chlorophyll. They are autotrophs. The zygote develops into an **embryo** that is protected and fed inside the parent plant. All plants show **alternation of generations**.", title="Kingdom Plantae"),
        D(r"**Alternation of generations** is a life cycle in which a diploid (2n) spore-producing body, the **sporophyte**, alternates with a haploid (n) gamete-producing body, the **gametophyte**.", title="Alternation of generations"),
        IMG("al-bil2-alternation.svg", alt="Cycle diagram: the haploid gametophyte makes gametes by mitosis; two gametes fuse at fertilisation to form the diploid zygote which grows into the sporophyte; the sporophyte makes haploid spores by meiosis; spores grow into gametophytes.", caption="Figure 1. Alternation of generations. Meiosis makes spores, not gametes."),
        MISTAKE("Meiosis makes gametes in plants", r"In animals, meiosis makes gametes. In plants, meiosis makes **spores**, and the gametophyte then makes gametes by **mitosis**. Remember: sporophyte, meiosis, spores; gametophyte, mitosis, gametes."),
        H("Bryophytes: mosses and liverworts"),
        T(r"Bryophytes (phylum Bryophyta in many books) include mosses and liverworts. Their features:"),
        KEY("Bryophyte features", "Small plants of damp places; they have no vascular tissue.", cards=[
            ["No xylem or phloem", "No lignified vessels, so water moves by diffusion and osmosis. This limits height to a few centimetres."],
            ["Rhizoids", "Hair-like threads anchor the plant and absorb a little water. They are not true roots."],
            ["Dominant gametophyte", "The leafy green plant you see is haploid. The sporophyte (a stalk, the seta, and a capsule) grows on it and depends on it."],
            ["Water for fertilisation", "Male gametes have flagella and swim in a film of water to the egg, so bryophytes need damp conditions."],
        ]),
        T(r"Life cycle of a moss: haploid spores (n) are released from the capsule and germinate into a thread-like **protonema**, which grows into a leafy gametophyte (n). The gametophyte makes **antheridia** (which release sperm) and **archegonia** (each holding one egg) by mitosis. A sperm swims to an egg: the zygote (2n) forms and grows in the archegonium into the sporophyte. Cells in the capsule divide by **meiosis** to make spores, and the capsule opens to release them."),
        H("Pteridophytes: ferns and their relatives"),
        T(r"Pteridophytes (called Filicinophyta, Pteridophyta or other names depending on the book: **check your textbook**) include ferns, horsetails and club mosses. Their main advance is **vascular tissue**: xylem strengthened with lignin carries water, and phloem carries sugars. This allows tall plants with true roots, stems and leaves."),
        KEY("Fern features", "The conspicuous green plant is now the sporophyte.", cards=[
            ["True organs", "Roots, a stem (often an underground rhizome) and leaves called fronds. Young fronds are coiled."],
            ["Vascular tissue", "Lignified xylem and phloem. Tree ferns can grow several metres tall."],
            ["Sori", "Brown dots under the fronds. Each sorus is a group of sporangia where meiosis makes haploid spores."],
            ["Tiny gametophyte", "The spore grows into a small, flat, heart-shaped, independent green prothallus that bears antheridia and archegonia."],
            ["Water for fertilisation", "Sperm still swim, so the prothallus must be wet when the sperm reach the egg."],
        ]),
        T(r"Life cycle of a fern: the sporophyte (2n) makes spores (n) by meiosis in the sporangia of the sori. A spore germinates into a prothallus (n). The prothallus makes sperm and eggs by mitosis. A sperm swims to the egg; the zygote (2n) grows on the prothallus into a young sporophyte, which becomes independent and grows into the large fern."),
        CODE("Feature                    Moss (Bryophyta)             Fern (Pteridophyta)\nDominant generation        gametophyte (n)              sporophyte (2n)\nVascular tissue            none                         xylem (lignified) and phloem\nRoots                      rhizoids only                true roots\nSporophyte                 dependent on gametophyte     independent\nGametophyte                leafy plant, long-lived      small prothallus\nSpore-bearing structure    capsule on a seta            sporangia in sori\nWater for fertilisation    needed                       needed", "text", title="Table 1. Moss and fern compared"),
        EX("Worked example 1: ploidy in the fern cycle", intro=r"A fern frond cell has 64 chromosomes. State the number in (a) a spore, (b) a prothallus cell, (c) the zygote.", steps=[
            S("Sporophyte is diploid", text="Frond cells are 2n = 64."),
            S("Spore is made by meiosis", text="Meiosis halves the number: spore n = 32."),
            S("The prothallus grows from a spore by mitosis", text="Prothallus cells are n = 32."),
            S("Zygote is formed by fusion of two gametes (each n = 32)", text="Zygote 2n = 64."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: why mosses stay small", intro=r"Explain why mosses rarely grow taller than a few centimetres but ferns can grow much taller.", steps=[
            S("Water transport in mosses", text="Mosses have no xylem. Water must pass cell to cell by osmosis and diffusion, which is slow over long distances."),
            S("Support in mosses", text="They have no lignin, so tall shoots would not stay upright."),
            S("Ferns", text="Lignified xylem carries water long distances and supports the stem, so tall plants are possible."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: identify the structure", intro=r"A student finds brown dots under a green frond. They are group structures releasing dust-like particles when ripe. Name the structures and the particles, and state how the particles are made.", steps=[
            S("Structure", text="Brown dots under the frond are sori, groups of sporangia."),
            S("Particles", text="The dust-like particles are spores."),
            S("How they are made", text="By meiosis in the sporangia, so the spores are haploid."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("In a moss, the plant you normally see as a green leafy cushion is:", ["The diploid sporophyte", "The haploid gametophyte", "A prothallus", "The zygote"], 1,
             "The dominant, leafy moss plant is the haploid gametophyte; the sporophyte (capsule on a stalk) grows on it.",
             hint="Which generation is dominant in bryophytes?",
             feedback=["In ferns the sporophyte is dominant, not in mosses.", "", "A prothallus is the gametophyte of a fern.", "A zygote is a single cell."]),
        QUIZ("Which statement about the fern life cycle is correct?", ["Gametes are made by meiosis in the prothallus", "Spores are made by mitosis in the sori", "Meiosis happens in the sporangia and the spores are haploid", "The zygote is haploid"], 2,
             "Sori contain sporangia where meiosis makes haploid spores.",
             hint="Where are the spores made and by which type of division?",
             feedback=["The prothallus makes gametes by mitosis, since it is already haploid.", "Spores are made by meiosis.", "", "The zygote is formed from two haploid gametes, so it is diploid."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"State **two** features of bryophytes that restrict them to damp habitats.", r"Their male gametes swim and need a film of water to reach the egg. They have no vascular tissue or waxy waterproof cuticle on the main plant, so water moves by diffusion and osmosis and the plant dries out easily. (They also have rhizoids, not true roots.)"),
        PQ(r"Describe how a moss sporophyte differs from a fern sporophyte.", r"The moss sporophyte (seta and capsule) is small, short-lived and depends on the gametophyte for water and food; it has no roots or leaves. The fern sporophyte is the large, independent plant with roots, stem, fronds and vascular tissue that photosynthesises for itself."),
        PQ(r"Explain what is meant by alternation of generations, and state where meiosis occurs in a fern.", r"The life cycle alternates between a diploid sporophyte, which makes spores, and a haploid gametophyte, which makes gametes. In a fern meiosis occurs in the sporangia, in the sori on the fronds of the sporophyte."),
        PQ(r"A fern spore germinated in dry soil and did not produce a new fern. Suggest a reason.", r"The prothallus makes motile (swimming) sperm, which need a film of water to reach the egg. In dry soil fertilisation cannot occur, so no zygote forms and no new sporophyte grows."),
        SUM(
            "Plants are multicellular, cellulose-walled, photosynthetic eukaryotes that protect the embryo and show alternation of generations.",
            "Sporophyte (2n) makes spores by meiosis; gametophyte (n) makes gametes by mitosis.",
            "Bryophytes: no vascular tissue, rhizoids, dominant gametophyte, dependent sporophyte, swimming sperm.",
            "Pteridophytes: lignified xylem and phloem, true roots and fronds, dominant sporophyte, sori, small independent prothallus, swimming sperm.",
            "Names of the plant phyla vary between books (for example Filicinophyta or Pteridophyta); check your textbook.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. Plantae 2: seed plants
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=TAX,
    topic="Plantae",
    title="Plantae II: seed plants, gymnosperms and flowering plants",
    slug="bil2-plantae-seed-plants",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Describe the features of Spermatophytes, compare gymnosperms with angiosperms and monocotyledons with dicotyledons, and explain the trends away from dependence on water.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the main features of seed plants",
        "Compare gymnosperms with angiosperms",
        "Compare monocotyledons with dicotyledons",
        "Explain the evolutionary trends in plants from mosses to flowering plants",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Spermatophyte", "Gymnosperm", "Angiosperm", "Monocotyledon", "Dicotyledon", "Pollen", "Seed", "Reduced gametophyte"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Oil palms, cocoa trees, maize, mangoes and the pines grown in forestry plantations are all seed plants. Seed plants (**Spermatophytes**) dominate the land today. Their success depends on two inventions that free reproduction from liquid water: **pollen** and **seeds**."),
        H("Features of seed plants"),
        KEY("What seed plants share", "All have xylem and phloem, roots, stems and leaves, and a dominant sporophyte.", cards=[
            ["Pollen", "The male gametophyte is a tiny pollen grain carried by wind or animals. The male gamete reaches the egg through a pollen tube, so no free water is needed."],
            ["Seed", "A seed holds an embryo and a food store inside a protective coat, and it can lie dormant until conditions suit germination."],
            ["Reduced gametophyte", "The gametophytes are only a few cells and live inside the sporophyte, protected and fed by it."],
            ["Secondary growth", "Many are woody and grow wider by the activity of a vascular cambium. (Monocotyledons generally lack it.)"],
        ]),
        T(r"Compare this with ferns: fern sperm swim in water, the gametophyte lives on the soil, and the spores are the dispersal unit. In seed plants the dispersal unit is the seed, which carries a ready-made embryo and food."),
        H("Gymnosperms and angiosperms"),
        D(r"**Gymnosperms** ('naked seed') are seed plants with seeds that are not enclosed in an ovary. They usually have cones. **Angiosperms** ('enclosed seed') have flowers, and their seeds develop inside an ovary that becomes a fruit.", title="Two groups of seed plants"),
        CODE("Feature            Gymnosperms (pine, cycad)        Angiosperms (flowering plants)\nReproductive part  cones                            flowers\nOvules             exposed on cone scales           enclosed in an ovary\nFruit              none                             fruit formed from the ovary\nPollination        mostly wind                      wind, insects, birds, bats\nFertilisation      single                           double (zygote and endosperm)\nWater transport    mainly tracheids                 vessels and tracheids\nPhloem             sieve cells                      sieve tube elements with companion cells\nExamples           Pinus, Cupressus                 oil palm, mango, maize", "text", title="Table 1. Gymnosperms and angiosperms"),
        T(r"The flower is the main reason for the diversity of angiosperms: it attracts animals that carry pollen accurately from one plant to another, and the fruit helps to disperse the seeds. In **double fertilisation** one male gamete fuses with the egg to give the diploid zygote, and the second fuses with two polar nuclei to give the triploid **endosperm**, the food store of the seed. The details of how the pollen and embryo sac form are met in Upper Sixth."),
        H("Monocotyledons and dicotyledons"),
        T(r"Angiosperms are traditionally divided by the number of seed leaves, or **cotyledons**, in the embryo. Modern DNA studies show that the dicotyledons are not a single natural group, but the two names are still used in A Level courses. **Check your textbook** for how it treats this."),
        CODE("Feature               Monocotyledons                 Dicotyledons\nCotyledons            one                            two\nLeaf veins            parallel                       net-like\nFlower parts          in threes or multiples         in fours or fives (or multiples)\nVascular bundles      scattered in the stem          in a ring\nCambium               absent (usually)               present (secondary growth)\nRoots                 fibrous                        tap root with branches\nExamples              maize, banana, oil palm        cocoa, mango, groundnut, cassava", "text", title="Table 2. Monocotyledons and dicotyledons"),
        MISTAKE("Using one feature only", r"Some exceptions exist (for example, some dicots have parallel veins). To identify the group, use several features together, and always name the feature you use in the answer."),
        H("Trends in the plant kingdom"),
        CODE("Trend                       Bryophyte -> Fern -> Conifer -> Flowering plant\nDominant generation         gametophyte -> sporophyte (all later groups)\nGametophyte size            large -> small -> tiny, inside sporophyte\nWater for fertilisation     needed -> needed -> not needed (pollen tube)\nVascular tissue             none -> xylem and phloem -> xylem and phloem\nDispersal unit              spore -> spore -> seed -> seed in fruit", "text", title="Table 3. Major trends in plants"),
        EX("Worked example 1: classify from features", intro=r"A plant has one cotyledon, parallel-veined leaves, flower parts in threes and scattered vascular bundles. It bears fruits in clusters. Name its group.", steps=[
            S("Count cotyledons", text="One cotyledon: monocotyledon."),
            S("Support with other features", text="Parallel veins, parts in threes and scattered bundles agree."),
            S("Choose the larger group", text="It has flowers and fruits, so it is an angiosperm. The plant is a monocotyledonous angiosperm (for example, banana or oil palm)."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: explain why pollen helped on land", intro=r"Explain why the evolution of pollen allowed plants to colonise drier habitats.", steps=[
            S("Fern or moss", text="Swimming sperm need a layer of water to reach the egg, so reproduction is limited to wet times and places."),
            S("Seed plant", text="The male gametophyte is a pollen grain, protected by a tough wall and carried by wind or animals to the female part."),
            S("Fertilisation", text="A pollen tube grows to the egg and delivers the male gametes, so no external water is needed."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: compare two seed groups", intro=r"State **three** differences between a pine and a mango tree in reproduction.", steps=[
            S("Reproductive structure", text="Pine has cones; mango has flowers."),
            S("Ovule protection", text="Pine ovules are exposed on scales; mango ovules are enclosed in an ovary."),
            S("Fruit", text="Pine forms no fruit; mango forms a fruit from the ovary, which aids seed dispersal by animals."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("Which feature is found in angiosperms but not in gymnosperms?", ["Pollen", "Seeds", "Ovules enclosed in an ovary", "Xylem"], 2,
             "Angiosperm ovules develop inside an ovary, which becomes the fruit.",
             hint="Gymnosperm means 'naked seed'.",
             feedback=["Both groups produce pollen.", "Both groups produce seeds.", "", "Both groups have xylem."]),
        QUIZ("A plant has net-veined leaves, flower parts in fives and vascular bundles in a ring. It is most likely a:", ["Monocotyledon", "Dicotyledon", "Moss", "Fern"], 1,
             "These features are typical of dicotyledons.",
             hint="Parallel veins and threes go with monocotyledons. Which group is left?",
             feedback=["Monocotyledons have parallel veins and scattered bundles.", "", "Mosses have no vascular bundles or flowers.", "Ferns have no flowers or seeds."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"State **two** ways in which the seed is an advantage to a plant over a spore.", r"A seed contains a food store (endosperm or cotyledons) that feeds the embryo until it can photosynthesise, and a protective coat. A seed can remain dormant until conditions are suitable, and in a fruit it can be dispersed by animals or wind. A spore contains little food and no embryo."),
        PQ(r"List **three** differences between monocotyledons and dicotyledons.", r"Monocots have one cotyledon, dicots two. Monocot leaves have parallel veins, dicot leaves net veins. Monocot vascular bundles are scattered in the stem; dicot bundles are in a ring. Monocots usually lack cambium, so there is no secondary growth; dicot roots are tap roots, monocot roots fibrous."),
        PQ(r"Explain why seed plants do not need liquid water for fertilisation.", r"The male gametophyte is a pollen grain carried by wind or animals to the female part. A pollen tube grows to the egg and carries the male gametes to it. No swimming sperm are involved."),
        PQ(r"Describe how the gametophyte generation changes from mosses to flowering plants.", r"In mosses the gametophyte is the large, leafy, dominant plant. In ferns it is a small, short-lived but independent prothallus. In seed plants it is reduced to a few cells (pollen grain and embryo sac) that live inside the sporophyte and depend on it."),
        SUM(
            "Seed plants have pollen, seeds, a reduced gametophyte and a dominant sporophyte with vascular tissue.",
            "Gymnosperms have cones and naked seeds; angiosperms have flowers, seeds enclosed in fruits and double fertilisation.",
            "Monocotyledons: one cotyledon, parallel veins, scattered bundles; dicotyledons: two cotyledons, net veins, bundles in a ring.",
            "From mosses to flowering plants the sporophyte becomes dominant, the gametophyte shrinks, and reproduction no longer needs free water.",
            "Textbooks differ in naming the groups and treat the monocot-dicot split differently; check your textbook.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6. Animalia I: body plans, sponges to annelids
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=TAX,
    topic="Animalia phyla",
    title="Animalia I: body plans and the phyla Porifera to Annelida",
    slug="bil2-animalia-sponges-to-annelids",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Describe the features of animals and the body-plan terms used to sort them, then identify Porifera, Cnidaria, Platyhelminthes, Nematoda and Annelida from their features.",
    objectives=[
        "State the features of the kingdom Animalia",
        "Use the terms symmetry, germ layers, body cavity and segmentation correctly",
        "Identify five invertebrate phyla from their characteristic features",
        "Link parasitic examples in Cameroon to their phylum",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Symmetry", "Diploblastic", "Triploblastic", "Coelom", "Acoelomate", "Segmentation", "Porifera", "Cnidaria", "Platyhelminthes", "Nematoda", "Annelida"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Most of the species that scientists have named are animals, and the great majority of these have no backbone. A farmer in the Western Highlands meets earthworms in the soil, a child near a stream in the East meets the flatworm that causes bilharzia, and a fisherman meets sponges tangled in his net. This lesson gives you a way to sort them, using the structure of the body."),
        H("Features of Animalia"),
        D(r"**Animalia** are multicellular eukaryotes whose cells have **no cell walls**. They are heterotrophs that **ingest** food and digest it internally. They store carbohydrate as glycogen, have nerve cells for coordination and, at some stage of the life cycle, can move. Most reproduce sexually, and the zygote develops into an embryo.", title="Kingdom Animalia"),
        H("Terms to describe a body plan"),
        KEY("Four questions that sort animals", "Ask these in turn when you meet a new animal.", cards=[
            ["Symmetry", "Asymmetric (no plane splits it into equal halves), radial (many planes through a central axis), or bilateral (only one plane gives a left and a right half)."],
            ["Germ layers", "Diploblastic animals have two layers of cells (ectoderm and endoderm). Triploblastic animals have three, with a mesoderm between them."],
            ["Body cavity", "Acoelomate: no fluid-filled cavity. Pseudocoelomate: a cavity not fully lined by mesoderm. Coelomate: a true coelom lined by mesoderm."],
            ["Segmentation", "The body is divided into repeating units along its length."],
        ]),
        TIP(r"Bilateral symmetry usually goes with a head at the front (cephalisation) and directional movement."),
        H("Porifera: sponges"),
        T(r"Sponges are aquatic and fixed to rocks (**sessile**). The body is a hollow sac with many pores through which water enters, and a large opening, the osculum, from which it leaves. They have **no true tissues or organs**. Collar cells (choanocytes) with flagella create the water current and trap food particles, so sponges are **filter feeders**. Chalky or glassy spikes called spicules (or spongin fibres) support the body. Sponges are asymmetric or radially symmetric."),
        H("Cnidaria: Hydra, jellyfish and corals"),
        T(r"Cnidarians are radially symmetric and diploblastic, with a jelly-like layer, the mesoglea, between the two cell layers. They have a gut with **one opening** that serves as both mouth and anus, surrounded by **tentacles** bearing stinging cells (**cnidocytes**) used to catch prey. Coordination is by a **nerve net**. The body is a sessile **polyp** (Hydra, sea anemone) or a free-swimming **medusa** (jellyfish); some species have both forms."),
        H("Platyhelminthes: flatworms"),
        T(r"Flatworms are bilaterally symmetrical, triploblastic and **acoelomate**. The body is flattened from top to bottom, so every cell is close to the surface for gaseous exchange by diffusion. The gut has one opening (or is absent, as in tapeworms). Many are parasites. *Schistosoma* (a fluke) causes bilharzia (schistosomiasis), spread by contact with water that contains infected freshwater snails; the tapeworm *Taenia* lives in the human intestine and absorbs digested food through its body surface."),
        H("Nematoda: roundworms"),
        T(r"Roundworms are cylindrical and unsegmented, with pointed ends. They are triploblastic and **pseudocoelomate**. Their gut has **two openings** (mouth and anus), and the body is covered by a tough **cuticle**. Muscles run only lengthwise, so they move with a whipping motion. Many are parasites: *Ascaris* in the intestine, *Onchocerca volvulus* (river blindness, spread by blackflies near fast rivers) and *Wuchereria* (elephantiasis)."),
        H("Annelida: segmented worms"),
        T(r"Annelids have bodies divided into many similar **segments**, a true **coelom**, a complete gut, a closed blood system and bristles called **chaetae** that grip the soil. Earthworms, ragworms and leeches are annelids. A true coelom acts as a hydrostatic skeleton and allows organs to grow independently of the body wall; segments allow flexible movement."),
        CODE("Phylum            Symmetry    Layers  Body cavity    Gut             Distinguishing feature\nPorifera          none/radial  none    none           none            pores, choanocytes, spicules\nCnidaria          radial      2       none           one opening     stinging cells, tentacles\nPlatyhelminthes   bilateral   3       acoelomate     one opening     flat, unsegmented body\nNematoda          bilateral   3       pseudocoelom   mouth + anus    cylindrical, tough cuticle\nAnnelida          bilateral   3       coelom         mouth + anus    segments, chaetae", "text", title="Table 1. Five invertebrate phyla"),
        EX("Worked example 1: place an unknown animal", intro=r"A worm from a pond is long, thin and bilaterally symmetrical. Its body is divided into many similar rings, and it has bristles. Name the phylum and give two reasons.", steps=[
            S("Check segmentation", text="Body rings (segments) point to Annelida."),
            S("Check chaetae", text="Bristles (chaetae) agree."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Phylum Annelida. A true coelom is expected; it can be confirmed by cutting the body."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: why a tapeworm has a flat body", intro=r"A tapeworm lives in the gut and has no gut of its own. Explain how its flat shape suits its way of life.", steps=[
            S("Feeding", text="It absorbs already-digested food through its surface, so a large surface area is useful."),
            S("Flat shape", text="A thin, long body has a large surface area to volume ratio."),
            S("Gas exchange", text="Oxygen (a little) and carbon dioxide diffuse across the surface; no cell is far from the surface, so no circulatory system is needed."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: choosing between two similar phyla", intro=r"A parasitic worm is long and cylindrical, not segmented, has a mouth and an anus and a tough cuticle. Why is it a nematode and not an annelid?", steps=[
            S("Check segmentation", text="Annelids have segments. This worm has none."),
            S("Check cuticle and body cavity", text="A tough cuticle and a pseudocoelom are features of nematodes."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The unsegmented body with a cuticle means Nematoda."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up flatworms and roundworms", r"Platyhelminthes are flat, with one gut opening and no body cavity. Nematodes are round in cross-section, with a mouth and an anus. Both are unsegmented, so do not use segmentation to separate them: use the shape and the gut."),
        QUIZ("Which phylum has stinging cells and a gut with only one opening?", ["Porifera", "Cnidaria", "Platyhelminthes", "Annelida"], 1,
             "Cnidarians have cnidocytes on their tentacles and a sac-like gut with one opening.",
             hint="Think of Hydra and jellyfish.",
             feedback=["Sponges have no gut or stinging cells.", "", "Flatworms have no stinging cells.", "Annelids have a complete gut with mouth and anus."]),
        QUIZ("A coelom is best described as:", ["A gut with two openings", "A fluid-filled body cavity lined with mesoderm", "A layer of jelly between two cell layers", "A hard outer cuticle"], 1,
             "A true coelom is a cavity completely lined with mesoderm; annelids, molluscs, arthropods and chordates have one.",
             hint="It is a type of body cavity.",
             feedback=["A gut with two openings is a complete digestive tract.", "", "That describes the mesoglea of cnidarians.", "A cuticle is a covering, not a cavity."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"State **three** features that distinguish Cnidaria from Platyhelminthes.", r"Cnidarians are radially symmetrical (flatworms bilateral), diploblastic (flatworms triploblastic), have cnidocytes (stinging cells) and tentacles, and a nerve net. They have a polyp or medusa body form."),
        PQ(r"Explain what is meant by *triploblastic*, and name a phylum that is not triploblastic.", r"A triploblastic animal has three embryonic cell layers: ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm. Cnidaria are diploblastic (two layers) and Porifera have no true layers."),
        PQ(r"A farmer finds a round, white worm in the intestine of a pig. It has a mouth, an anus and a tough cuticle and is not segmented. Name its phylum and give the type of body cavity.", r"Phylum Nematoda (roundworm). The body cavity is a pseudocoelom, a cavity that is not completely lined by mesoderm."),
        PQ(r"Describe **two** advantages of a segmented body in an earthworm.", r"Segments allow the muscles in each section to contract independently, giving flexible, controlled movement like burrowing. The coelom in each segment acts as a hydrostatic skeleton, giving support. Segments also allow growth by adding segments and, with the coelom, let organs develop separately from the body wall."),
        SUM(
            "Animals are multicellular heterotrophs with no cell walls; they ingest food and have nerve cells.",
            "Sort animals by symmetry, number of germ layers, body cavity and segmentation.",
            "Porifera: pores, choanocytes, no tissues. Cnidaria: radial, stinging cells, one gut opening.",
            "Platyhelminthes: flat, acoelomate. Nematoda: round, pseudocoelom, cuticle, mouth and anus.",
            "Annelida: segmented, coelomate, chaetae, closed blood system.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7. Animalia II: molluscs, arthropods, chordates
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=TAX,
    topic="Animalia phyla",
    title="Animalia II: Mollusca, Arthropoda and Chordata",
    slug="bil2-animalia-molluscs-arthropods-chordates",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Identify molluscs, the arthropod classes and the vertebrate classes from their features, and use the chordate characters to explain why vertebrates are grouped as they are.",
    objectives=[
        "State the features of Mollusca and name its main classes",
        "Distinguish insects, arachnids, crustaceans and myriapods",
        "State the four features of chordates",
        "Distinguish the five vertebrate classes",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Mollusca", "Arthropoda", "Exoskeleton", "Tagmata", "Chordata", "Notochord", "Vertebrata", "Mammalia"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Snails are sold in markets in Yaounde, crayfish and shrimps on the coast, and the fish, chickens and goats in every home are chordates. The three largest animal phyla in everyday life are Mollusca, Arthropoda and Chordata. The arthropods alone contain more known species than all other phyla together."),
        H("Mollusca"),
        T(r"Molluscs have a soft, unsegmented body with a muscular **foot** for movement, a **mantle** (a fold of tissue that secretes the shell, where there is one), and usually a rasping tongue called the **radula**. They have a coelom (reduced in adults), and gills or a lung."),
        KEY("Three classes", "Distinguish them by shell and head.", cards=[
            ["Gastropoda", "A single coiled shell (or none) and a distinct head with tentacles. Snails, slugs, whelks (the giant African land snail Achatina is a gastropod)."],
            ["Bivalvia", "Two hinged shell valves, no distinct head, no radula. Mussels, oysters; filter feeders."],
            ["Cephalopoda", "Foot modified into tentacles around the head; a closed blood system and large brain. Squid, octopus."],
        ]),
        H("Arthropoda"),
        D(r"**Arthropoda** have a body divided into segments, **jointed limbs** and a hard **exoskeleton** of chitin. The exoskeleton cannot grow, so the animal **moults** (ecdysis). The body cavity is a **haemocoel** and the blood circulates in an **open circulatory system**.", title="Arthropoda"),
        T(r"The exoskeleton protects against water loss and enemies, and gives muscles a firm anchor. The segments are often fused into regions called **tagmata** (head, thorax, abdomen). Four main classes are recognised by counting legs and body regions."),
        CODE("Class        Body regions             Legs         Antennae   Wings       Examples\nInsecta      head, thorax, abdomen    3 pairs      1 pair     usually 1-2 pairs   housefly, mosquito, termite\nArachnida    cephalothorax, abdomen   4 pairs      none       none        spider, scorpion, tick\nCrustacea    cephalothorax, abdomen   5+ pairs     2 pairs    none        crab, prawn, crayfish\nMyriapoda    head and long trunk      many         1 pair     none        centipede, millipede", "text", title="Table 1. Arthropod classes"),
        MISTAKE("Calling a spider an insect", r"Spiders have eight legs, two body regions and no antennae. Insects have six legs, three body regions and one pair of antennae. Count the legs first."),
        T(r"Millipedes (Diplopoda) have two pairs of legs on most segments; centipedes (Chilopoda) have one pair. Some books put these in two separate classes, others in one class Myriapoda: **check your textbook**."),
        H("Chordata"),
        D(r"Chordates have, at some stage of their life, a **notochord** (a stiff rod along the back), a **dorsal hollow nerve cord**, **pharyngeal slits** (gill slits) in the throat and a **post-anal tail**.", title="Four chordate features"),
        T(r"In vertebrates (subphylum Vertebrata) the notochord is replaced by a backbone (vertebral column) of bone or cartilage, and the nerve cord is the spinal cord, protected by it. Pharyngeal slits become gills in fish and are present only in the embryo in land vertebrates."),
        CODE("Class       Skin covering   Gas exchange        Heart       Temp.        Reproduction\nFish        wet scales      gills               2 chambers  ectotherm    external fertilisation, eggs in water\nAmphibia    moist, no scales  skin, lungs, gills (larva)  3 chambers  ectotherm  external fertilisation, eggs in water\nReptilia    dry scales      lungs               3 chambers* ectotherm    internal fert., shelled eggs on land\nAves        feathers        lungs + air sacs    4 chambers  endotherm    internal fert., shelled eggs\nMammalia    hair            lungs, diaphragm    4 chambers  endotherm    internal fert., young fed with milk\n* crocodiles have four", "text", title="Table 2. The five vertebrate classes"),
        T(r"Mammals have hair, mammary glands that produce milk, a diaphragm and an external ear flap (pinna) in most species. Most mammals give birth to live young; the platypus and echidna lay eggs. Amphibians, such as frogs, usually have an aquatic larva (tadpole) that undergoes **metamorphosis**, so they need water for reproduction."),
        EX("Worked example 1: classify an arthropod", intro=r"A specimen has a head, thorax and abdomen, three pairs of legs, one pair of antennae and two pairs of wings. Name the class and one reason it is not a crustacean.", steps=[
            S("Count the legs and body regions", text="Three pairs of legs and three body regions: Insecta."),
            S("Check antennae and wings", text="One pair of antennae and wings agree with Insecta."),
            S("Crustacean check", text="Crustaceans have two pairs of antennae and five or more pairs of legs."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: classify a vertebrate", intro=r"A vertebrate has dry scaly skin, lungs, a three-chambered heart and lays leathery-shelled eggs on land. It is an ectotherm. Name its class.", steps=[
            S("Skin and temperature", text="Dry scales and ectothermy rule out Amphibia (moist skin), Aves (feathers) and Mammalia (hair)."),
            S("Egg", text="A shelled egg laid on land, with internal fertilisation, fits the reptiles."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Class Reptilia."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: why mammals can live in colder habitats than reptiles", intro=r"Use Table 2 to give two reasons.", steps=[
            S("Temperature control", text="Mammals are endotherms: they produce heat inside the body, so their activity does not fall when the air cools."),
            S("Insulation and circulation", text="Hair traps air for insulation, and the four-chambered heart keeps oxygenated and deoxygenated blood separate, supplying oxygen fast for heat production."),
            S("Contrast", text="Reptiles depend on external heat, so they become inactive in cold."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("Which of these is a chordate feature that humans have only as an embryo?", ["Dorsal hollow nerve cord", "Pharyngeal slits", "Hair", "Four-chambered heart"], 1,
             "Pharyngeal slits and the post-anal tail appear in the human embryo and then disappear or are modified.",
             hint="The nerve cord stays for life.",
             feedback=["The spinal cord is the dorsal hollow nerve cord and stays for life.", "", "Hair is a mammalian feature, not one of the four chordate features.", "Heart chambers are not a chordate characteristic."]),
        QUIZ("An animal has eight legs, two body regions and no antennae. It is a member of the class:", ["Insecta", "Crustacea", "Arachnida", "Myriapoda"], 2,
             "Eight legs, a cephalothorax and abdomen and no antennae are the features of Arachnida.",
             hint="Compare with Table 1.",
             feedback=["Insects have six legs and three body regions.", "Crustaceans have two pairs of antennae.", "", "Myriapods have many legs and a long trunk."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"State **three** features of the phylum Arthropoda.", r"Jointed limbs, a chitin exoskeleton that is moulted as the animal grows, a segmented body (often with fused segments in tagmata), an open circulatory system with a haemocoel."),
        PQ(r"Explain why arthropods must moult but vertebrates do not.", r"The arthropod exoskeleton is rigid and cannot expand, so to grow the animal must shed it and make a larger one (ecdysis). Vertebrates have an internal skeleton that grows with the body."),
        PQ(r"Name the class of vertebrate with each description: (a) feathers and a four-chambered heart, (b) moist skin and a tadpole larva, (c) gills and wet scales.", r"(a) Aves; (b) Amphibia; (c) Fish (Pisces or the fish classes, depending on the textbook)."),
        PQ(r"Give **two** reasons why a snail is placed in Mollusca and not in Annelida.", r"A snail has a muscular foot, a mantle that secretes a shell, and a radula, and its body is unsegmented. Annelids have a body divided into segments with chaetae and no shell."),
        SUM(
            "Mollusca: soft unsegmented body, muscular foot, mantle, usually a shell and radula; classes Gastropoda, Bivalvia, Cephalopoda.",
            "Arthropoda: jointed limbs, chitin exoskeleton, moulting, open circulation; classes by leg and body region count.",
            "Chordata: notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord, pharyngeal slits, post-anal tail at some stage.",
            "Vertebrate classes differ in skin, gas exchange, heart chambers, temperature control and reproduction.",
            "Whether myriapods form one class or two varies between books; check your textbook.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8. Chloroplast structure and pigments
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=NUT,
    topic="Chloroplast structure and pigments",
    title="The chloroplast, photosynthetic pigments and spectra",
    slug="bil2-chloroplast-pigments-spectra",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Relate chloroplast structure to its role, explain the roles of the pigments, and interpret absorption and action spectra and chromatography Rf values.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the ultrastructure of a chloroplast and the function of each part",
        "Explain the roles of chlorophyll a, chlorophyll b and the carotenoids",
        "Interpret absorption and action spectra and explain why they differ slightly",
        "Separate pigments by paper chromatography and calculate Rf values",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Chloroplast", "Granum", "Thylakoid", "Stroma", "Chlorophyll a", "Accessory pigment", "Absorption spectrum", "Action spectrum", "Rf value"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Every cocoa farm, banana plantation and maize field in Cameroon runs on one process: **photosynthesis**, in which light energy is converted into the chemical energy of organic molecules. In plants and algae it takes place in organelles called **chloroplasts**. The overall equation is:"),
        F(r"6\,\mathrm{CO_2} + 6\,\mathrm{H_2O} \xrightarrow{\text{light, chlorophyll}} \mathrm{C_6H_{12}O_6} + 6\,\mathrm{O_2}"),
        H("Chloroplast ultrastructure"),
        T(r"A typical chloroplast is a flattened oval a few micrometres long. It is surrounded by an **envelope** of two membranes. Inside is a gel-like **stroma** and a system of flattened membrane sacs called **thylakoids**."),
        KEY("Parts and functions", "Structure fits function: the light reactions and the Calvin cycle are held in different places.", cards=[
            ["Envelope", "Two membranes; the inner membrane controls which substances enter and leave the stroma."],
            ["Thylakoid membranes", "Hold the photosystems, electron carriers and ATP synthase. Light-dependent reactions occur here. The large total membrane area holds many pigment molecules."],
            ["Grana", "Stacks of thylakoids (singular granum). Stacking packs a large area of membrane into a small volume. Thin lamellae join grana together."],
            ["Stroma", "Contains the enzymes of the Calvin cycle (including rubisco), starch grains, lipid droplets, circular DNA and 70S ribosomes. Light-independent reactions occur here."],
            ["Thylakoid space", "The inside of a thylakoid. H+ ions build up here during the light stage and make a concentration gradient."],
        ]),
        T(r"The presence of DNA and 70S ribosomes in the stroma allows chloroplasts to make some of their own proteins, and supports the theory that chloroplasts arose from cyanobacteria taken into an early eukaryote cell (the endosymbiotic theory)."),
        H("Photosynthetic pigments"),
        D(r"A **pigment** is a molecule that absorbs some wavelengths of light and reflects or transmits the others. **Chlorophylls** and **carotenoids** are the photosynthetic pigments. They are arranged in **photosystems** in the thylakoid membrane.", title="Pigment"),
        CODE("Pigment             Colour          Role\nChlorophyll a       blue-green      primary pigment: found in the reaction centre\nChlorophyll b       yellow-green    accessory pigment: passes energy to chlorophyll a\nCarotene            orange          accessory pigment; protects against too much light\nXanthophyll         yellow          accessory pigment; protects against too much light", "text", title="Table 1. The main pigments"),
        T(r"In a **photosystem**, many accessory pigments act as an **antenna**, absorbing light and passing the energy towards one special chlorophyll a molecule at the **reaction centre**. This is where the energy is used to excite an electron. Accessory pigments widen the range of wavelengths that can be used, and carotenoids also protect the chlorophyll from damage by bright light."),
        H("Absorption and action spectra"),
        D(r"An **absorption spectrum** is a graph of how much light of each wavelength is absorbed by a pigment (measured with a colorimeter on an extract). An **action spectrum** is a graph of the rate of photosynthesis (for example, oxygen released) at each wavelength of light.", title="Two spectra"),
        IMG("al-bil2-absorption-spectra.svg", alt="Graph of absorbance against wavelength 400 to 700 nm: chlorophyll a and b peak in blue-violet and red, carotenoids absorb blue only, and the action spectrum follows the combined absorption with a dip in green.", caption="Figure 1. Schematic absorption spectra of pigments and the action spectrum (curves are drawn for teaching, not from measurements)."),
        T(r"Both chlorophylls absorb strongly in the **blue-violet** (about 430 to 470 nm) and **red** (about 640 to 670 nm) parts of the spectrum and absorb green light weakly, which is why leaves look green: green light is reflected and transmitted. Carotenoids absorb blue light (roughly 450 to 500 nm) and reflect yellow and orange."),
        T(r"The action spectrum follows the shape of the combined absorption spectra. This is evidence that the pigments absorb the light that drives photosynthesis. It is not an exact copy: light absorbed by carotenoids is passed on less efficiently than light absorbed by chlorophyll, and some green light is still used, so the action spectrum is slightly different from the absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a alone."),
        T(r"In Engelmann's experiment (1880s) a thread of the alga *Spirogyra* was lit by a spectrum of light, in a drop of water containing aerobic bacteria. The bacteria gathered where most oxygen was released, in the blue and red regions. This was an early action spectrum."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the two spectra", r"The **absorption** spectrum is for the pigment (how much light it takes in). The **action** spectrum is for the whole process (how fast photosynthesis goes). Examination graphs label the vertical axis, so read it first: absorbance, or rate of photosynthesis."),
        H("Separating the pigments"),
        T(r"In **paper chromatography** a leaf extract in propanone is placed as a small spot near the bottom of a strip of chromatography paper, which is then stood in a solvent. As the solvent rises, pigments move at different speeds, depending on how soluble they are in the solvent and how strongly they stick to the paper. Carotene is most soluble and travels furthest; chlorophyll b travels least."),
        D(r"The **Rf value** of a pigment is the distance moved by the pigment divided by the distance moved by the solvent front, both measured from the origin line. Rf is always between 0 and 1.", title="Rf value", formula=r"R_f = \dfrac{\text{distance moved by pigment}}{\text{distance moved by solvent front}}"),
        EX("Worked example 1: calculating Rf", intro=r"The solvent front moved 8.0 cm from the origin. Four pigment spots were centred at 7.6 cm, 6.0 cm, 5.2 cm and 3.6 cm. Calculate the Rf value of each and name the pigment that moved furthest (invented teaching data).", steps=[
            S("Use the formula for each spot", math=r"R_f = \dfrac{7.6}{8.0} = 0.95,\quad \dfrac{6.0}{8.0} = 0.75,\quad \dfrac{5.2}{8.0} = 0.65,\quad \dfrac{3.6}{8.0} = 0.45"),
            S("Identify the order", text="Carotene is most soluble, so it has the highest Rf (0.95). Next xanthophyll (0.75), chlorophyll a (0.65) and chlorophyll b (0.45)."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: why leaves look green", intro=r"Use Figure 1 to explain why leaves look green and why a plant under pure green light grows poorly.", steps=[
            S("Absorption", text="Chlorophylls absorb red and blue strongly; carotenoids absorb blue. Little green light is absorbed."),
            S("Reflection", text="The green light that is not absorbed is reflected and transmitted to our eyes."),
            S("Growth under green light", text="The action spectrum is low in green. Little light is absorbed, so photosynthesis is slow."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: interpreting an experiment", intro=r"A student puts discs of leaf under blue, green and red filters of equal light intensity. Oxygen output per hour is highest under red, a little less under blue and lowest under green. Explain.", steps=[
            S("Link to the action spectrum", text="Red and blue are absorbed by chlorophyll, so many photons drive the light-dependent stage."),
            S("Green", text="Green is poorly absorbed, so less energy reaches the reaction centres."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The results match the action spectrum."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("Which colour of light is least effective for photosynthesis in a green leaf?", ["Red", "Blue", "Green", "Violet"], 2,
             "Green light is reflected and transmitted, so it is the least absorbed.",
             hint="Which colour of light do leaves reflect?",
             feedback=["Chlorophyll absorbs red strongly.", "Chlorophyll absorbs blue strongly.", "", "Violet is at the blue end, where chlorophyll absorbs well."]),
        QUIZ("In a chromatogram, pigment X moved 3.0 cm and the solvent front moved 6.0 cm. The Rf value of X is:", ["0.5", "2.0", "3.0", "18"], 0,
             "Rf = 3.0 / 6.0 = 0.5.",
             hint="Divide the pigment distance by the solvent distance.",
             feedback=["", "That is the solvent distance divided by the pigment distance, which is upside down.", "That is the distance moved, not the ratio.", "That is the two numbers multiplied."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"State **two** ways in which the structure of a chloroplast is adapted for photosynthesis.", r"Thylakoids stacked into grana give a very large membrane area for pigments, electron carriers and ATP synthase. The stroma holds all the Calvin cycle enzymes, close to the thylakoids where ATP and NADPH are made. Thylakoid spaces are small, so a proton gradient can build quickly."),
        PQ(r"Explain the difference between the role of chlorophyll a and accessory pigments in a photosystem.", r"Accessory pigments (chlorophyll b and carotenoids) absorb light of different wavelengths and pass the energy to the reaction centre. Chlorophyll a at the reaction centre is the primary pigment, which uses the energy to release an excited electron. Accessory pigments widen the range of light used."),
        PQ(r"Explain why the action spectrum of photosynthesis is similar to, but not identical with, the absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a.", r"Other pigments (chlorophyll b, carotenoids) also absorb light and pass energy to chlorophyll a, so the action spectrum shows the combined effect. Not all absorbed energy is transferred equally (carotenoids pass less), so the shapes differ a little."),
        PQ(r"In a chromatogram of a leaf extract the solvent front moved 9.0 cm. A yellow spot moved 6.3 cm. Calculate its Rf value.", r"Rf = 6.3 / 9.0 = 0.70. A yellow spot with Rf near 0.7 is consistent with xanthophyll (identify using the Rf of known pigments)."),
        SUM(
            "Chloroplast: double envelope, stroma with Calvin cycle enzymes, DNA and 70S ribosomes, thylakoids stacked into grana with photosystems.",
            "Chlorophyll a is at the reaction centre; chlorophyll b and carotenoids are accessory pigments that widen the range of light absorbed and protect.",
            "Absorption spectrum: light absorbed by a pigment. Action spectrum: rate of photosynthesis per wavelength. They match closely but not exactly.",
            "Leaves look green because green light is not absorbed.",
            "Rf = distance moved by pigment / distance moved by solvent front; carotene has the highest Rf.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9. Light-dependent stage
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=NUT,
    topic="The light-dependent stage",
    title="The light-dependent stage: the Z-scheme",
    slug="bil2-light-dependent-stage-z-scheme",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Trace electrons through photosystems II and I in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, explain photolysis and chemiosmosis in the thylakoid, and contrast cyclic with non-cyclic photophosphorylation.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the sequence of events in non-cyclic photophosphorylation using the Z-scheme",
        "Explain how a proton gradient makes ATP in the chloroplast",
        "Describe photolysis and the source of the oxygen released",
        "Compare cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Photosystem II", "Photosystem I", "Photolysis", "Electron transport chain", "Chemiosmosis", "ATP synthase", "NADPH", "Cyclic photophosphorylation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"The light-dependent stage turns light energy into two chemical products that the Calvin cycle can use: **ATP** and **reduced NADP** (NADPH). It also releases oxygen as a by-product, which every animal in a Cameroonian forest breathes. The stage takes place in the **thylakoid membranes**."),
        H("Photosystems"),
        D(r"A **photosystem** is a cluster of pigment molecules and proteins in the thylakoid membrane. The reaction centre of **photosystem II (PSII)** contains chlorophyll a that absorbs best near 680 nm (P680). The reaction centre of **photosystem I (PSI)** absorbs best near 700 nm (P700).", title="Photosystems"),
        TIP(r"The numbering is historical: PSII works *before* PSI in the electron flow. Do not let the numbers set the order."),
        H("Non-cyclic photophosphorylation"),
        D(r"**Photophosphorylation** is the making of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate (Pi) using energy from light. In **non-cyclic photophosphorylation** electrons flow in a line from water to NADP, and ATP, NADPH and oxygen are all produced.", title="Non-cyclic photophosphorylation"),
        IMG("al-bil2-z-scheme.svg", alt="Z-scheme: electrons from water pass to photosystem II, are raised in energy by light, pass down an electron transport chain releasing energy used to make ATP, are raised again at photosystem I by light and finally reduce NADP to NADPH.", caption="Figure 1. The Z-scheme. The vertical axis is energy level of the electron (higher is more energetic). Light lifts the electron at each photosystem."),
        CODE("1  Light is absorbed by PSII; an electron from P680 is raised to a higher energy level\n   and is captured by a primary electron acceptor.\n2  Photolysis: water is split to replace the lost electron.\n   2 H2O -> 4 H+ + 4 e- + O2\n3  The electron passes down an electron transport chain\n   (plastoquinone, cytochrome b6f complex, plastocyanin) losing energy.\n4  Energy released pumps H+ from the stroma into the thylakoid space.\n5  Light is absorbed by PSI; an electron from P700 is raised again\n   and is captured by a primary acceptor.\n6  The electron from the chain replaces the one lost from P700.\n7  The high-energy electron passes via ferredoxin to NADP reductase:\n   NADP+ + 2 e- + H+ -> NADPH\n8  H+ flows from the thylakoid space through ATP synthase into the\n   stroma: ADP + Pi -> ATP (chemiosmosis).", "text", title="Steps in non-cyclic photophosphorylation"),
        T(r"**Chemiosmosis** is the movement of H+ ions down their concentration gradient through ATP synthase, which uses the energy to make ATP. The gradient is built by three things: protons pumped across the thylakoid membrane by the electron transport chain, protons released into the thylakoid space by photolysis, and protons removed from the stroma when NADP is reduced. The thylakoid membrane is impermeable to H+ except through ATP synthase. The ATP is made on the stroma side, where the Calvin cycle uses it."),
        D(r"**Photolysis** is the splitting of water by light-driven action at PSII. The electrons replace those lost from the reaction centre, the H+ ions help build the proton gradient and later reduce NADP, and the oxygen is a waste gas that leaves through the stomata.", title="Photolysis"),
        T(r"Evidence that the oxygen comes from water, not carbon dioxide, includes experiments using water labelled with the heavy isotope $^{18}$O: the oxygen released was labelled, but when only the CO$_2$ was labelled it was not. In the **Hill reaction**, isolated chloroplasts in light reduce a dye such as DCPIP (blue to colourless) and release oxygen even without CO$_2$, which shows that the light stage does not depend on the Calvin cycle."),
        H("Cyclic photophosphorylation"),
        T(r"In **cyclic photophosphorylation** only PSI is used. The excited electron from P700 does not go to NADP. It passes along a chain of carriers (ferredoxin, the cytochrome complex, plastocyanin) and returns to P700. The energy lost on the way pumps H+ and makes ATP."),
        CODE("Feature             Non-cyclic                       Cyclic\nPhotosystems        PSII and PSI                     PSI only\nPhotolysis of water yes                              no\nOxygen released     yes                              no\nNADPH formed        yes                              no\nATP formed          yes                              yes\nElectron source     water                            P700 itself (electron returns)\nWhere electron ends NADP                             back to PSI", "text", title="Table 1. Cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation"),
        T(r"Why have both? The Calvin cycle needs more ATP than NADPH (3 ATP to 2 NADPH per CO$_2$ fixed), and the non-cyclic route does not make enough ATP on its own. Cyclic photophosphorylation tops up ATP when needed. It also continues when NADP is not available because the Calvin cycle is running slowly."),
        EX("Worked example 1: electrons, NADPH and oxygen", intro=r"Using $2\,\mathrm{H_2O} \to 4\,\mathrm{H^+} + 4\,\mathrm{e^-} + \mathrm{O_2}$ and that one NADPH takes two electrons, calculate how many NADPH are made for each O$_2$ released, and how many O$_2$ for each CO$_2$ fixed if the Calvin cycle needs 2 NADPH per CO$_2$.", steps=[
            S("Electrons from one O2", math=r"2\,\mathrm{H_2O} \rightarrow \mathrm{O_2} + 4\,\mathrm{H^+} + 4\,\mathrm{e^-}"),
            S("Two electrons per NADPH", math=r"4\,\mathrm{e^-} \div 2 = 2\ \text{NADPH per O}_2"),
            S("Calvin cycle needs 2 NADPH per CO2", math=r"2\ \text{NADPH} \Rightarrow 1\ \mathrm{O_2}\ \text{per}\ \mathrm{CO_2}"),
            S("Compare with the overall equation", text="6 CO2 fixed gives 6 O2, which is the 1 : 1 ratio in the equation."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: effect of an inhibitor", intro=r"The herbicide DCMU blocks electron flow from photosystem II to the electron transport chain. Predict the effect on oxygen release, ATP made by non-cyclic photophosphorylation and NADPH.", steps=[
            S("Electron flow is blocked after PSII", text="Electrons cannot reach the chain or PSI, so PSI receives none."),
            S("Photolysis", text="PSII cannot pass electrons on, so it cannot accept new ones from water and photolysis stops: oxygen release stops."),
            S("ATP and NADPH", text="No electron flow through the chain means no H+ pumping and no NADP reduction: non-cyclic ATP and NADPH fall to zero. Cyclic photophosphorylation can still make some ATP."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: proton gradient", intro=r"A scientist moves isolated thylakoids from pH 7 to a pH 4 solution in the dark, then to pH 8 with ADP and Pi. ATP is made without light. Explain.", steps=[
            S("Soak in acid", text="The thylakoid space becomes acidic (high H+) compared with the outside."),
            S("Move to pH 8", text="The outside now has low H+, so a steep gradient exists across the membrane."),
            S("ATP synthase", text="H+ flows down the gradient through ATP synthase, which makes ATP from ADP and Pi. This supports the chemiosmotic theory."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying that oxygen comes from carbon dioxide", r"The oxygen released in photosynthesis comes from the splitting of **water** at PSII. The oxygen atoms of CO$_2$ end up in carbohydrate and in water made in the Calvin cycle."),
        QUIZ("What is the final electron acceptor in non-cyclic photophosphorylation?", ["Oxygen", "Water", "NADP", "ADP"], 2,
             "The electron finally reduces NADP to NADPH, via ferredoxin.",
             hint="Which molecule carries the reducing power to the Calvin cycle?",
             feedback=["Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in respiration, not photosynthesis.", "Water is the electron donor, not the acceptor.", "", "ADP is phosphorylated to ATP; it does not accept electrons."]),
        QUIZ("Which product is **not** made in cyclic photophosphorylation?", ["ATP", "NADPH", "Proton gradient", "Excited electrons"], 1,
             "In the cyclic pathway electrons return to PSI, so NADP is not reduced. ATP is made.",
             hint="Where do the electrons go in the cyclic pathway?",
             feedback=["ATP is made.", "", "A proton gradient is made as the electrons pass the chain.", "Electrons are excited at PSI."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"Describe how a proton gradient is formed across the thylakoid membrane in non-cyclic photophosphorylation.", r"Photolysis releases H+ into the thylakoid space. The energy released by electrons passing along the transport chain is used to pump H+ from the stroma into the thylakoid space. H+ are also used from the stroma to reduce NADP, lowering stroma H+. So the thylakoid space has a higher H+ concentration than the stroma."),
        PQ(r"State the roles of photosystem II and photosystem I in non-cyclic photophosphorylation.", r"PSII absorbs light, loses an excited electron to the chain, and is the site of photolysis so that its lost electrons are replaced. PSI absorbs light and raises the electron (that arrives from the chain) to a high energy level, from which it reduces NADP."),
        PQ(r"Explain why cyclic photophosphorylation does not release oxygen.", r"Oxygen comes from photolysis of water at PSII. In the cyclic pathway PSII is not used and electrons are recycled to PSI, so no water is split and no oxygen is released."),
        PQ(r"Suggest why a plant would increase the rate of cyclic photophosphorylation when the Calvin cycle needs more ATP.", r"The Calvin cycle needs 3 ATP and 2 NADPH per CO2 fixed, a higher ratio of ATP than non-cyclic photophosphorylation provides. The cyclic route makes extra ATP without making NADPH or using water, so it adjusts the ratio."),
        SUM(
            "PSII (P680) and PSI (P700) are in the thylakoid membrane; electrons flow from water through PSII, a chain and PSI to NADP.",
            "Photolysis: 2 H2O gives 4 H+, 4 e- and O2; the electrons replace those lost by PSII.",
            "Chemiosmosis: an electron transport chain and photolysis build an H+ gradient; H+ flows through ATP synthase to make ATP.",
            "Non-cyclic makes ATP, NADPH and O2; cyclic uses PSI only and makes ATP only.",
            "Per CO2 fixed the Calvin cycle needs 3 ATP and 2 NADPH, so cyclic ATP supply helps to balance the ratio.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 10. Calvin cycle and photorespiration
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=NUT,
    topic="The Calvin cycle and photorespiration",
    title="The Calvin cycle and photorespiration",
    slug="bil2-calvin-cycle-photorespiration",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Describe fixation, reduction and regeneration in the Calvin cycle, count the carbon, ATP and NADPH needed to make glucose, and explain why rubisco's oxygenase activity makes photorespiration wasteful.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the three stages of the Calvin cycle in the stroma",
        "Calculate the ATP and NADPH needed for each CO2 fixed and for one glucose",
        "Predict how the amounts of GP and RuBP change when light or CO2 is removed",
        "Explain what photorespiration is and why it lowers the yield of C3 plants",
    ],
    key_concepts=["RuBP", "Rubisco", "GP", "Triose phosphate", "Carbon fixation", "Regeneration", "Photorespiration", "Oxygenase activity"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"The light-dependent stage makes ATP and NADPH, but a plant cannot eat ATP. Its growth, the starch in a yam and the sugar in sugarcane all come from the **Calvin cycle**, which fixes carbon dioxide into sugars. It is the main route of carbon into living things. It takes place in the **stroma** of the chloroplast and is called the light-independent stage because it does not use light directly. In practice it stops in the dark, because it needs ATP and NADPH from the light stage."),
        H("The three stages"),
        D(r"**Carbon fixation**: CO$_2$ is joined to the five-carbon sugar **ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP)** by the enzyme **rubisco**. The six-carbon product is unstable and splits at once into two molecules of the three-carbon compound **glycerate 3-phosphate (GP)**.", title="1. Fixation", formula=r"\mathrm{RuBP}\ (5\mathrm{C}) + \mathrm{CO_2} \xrightarrow{\text{rubisco}} 2\,\mathrm{GP}\ (3\mathrm{C})"),
        D(r"**Reduction**: GP is phosphorylated using ATP, then reduced using NADPH to give **triose phosphate (TP)**, also called glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (GALP or G3P). This uses ATP and NADPH from the light stage.", title="2. Reduction", formula=r"\mathrm{GP} \xrightarrow[\text{ATP}\to\text{ADP + Pi}]{\text{NADPH}\to\text{NADP}^+} \mathrm{TP}"),
        D(r"**Regeneration**: most of the triose phosphate is used to remake RuBP, using ATP. The rest leaves the cycle to make sugars and other organic molecules.", title="3. Regeneration of RuBP"),
        IMG("al-bil2-calvin-cycle.svg", alt="Diagram of the Calvin cycle with numbers for six turns: 6 CO2 join 6 RuBP to give 12 GP; 12 GP are reduced to 12 triose phosphate using 12 ATP and 12 NADPH; 2 triose phosphate leave to make one hexose and 10 are used to regenerate 6 RuBP using 6 ATP.", caption="Figure 1. The Calvin cycle for six turns: one hexose (glucose) is made. Numbers show molecules; carbon atoms balance at every step."),
        H("Counting carbon, ATP and NADPH"),
        T(r"One turn of the cycle fixes one CO$_2$. Because each triose phosphate has three carbons and a hexose has six, **six turns** are needed to make one hexose without losing RuBP. Count the molecules in Figure 1:"),
        CODE("Carbon fixation:   6 CO2 + 6 RuBP (30 C)      -> 12 GP (36 C)\nReduction:         12 GP + 12 ATP + 12 NADPH -> 12 TP (36 C)\nOutput:             2 TP (6 C)                -> 1 hexose\nRegeneration:      10 TP (30 C) + 6 ATP      -> 6 RuBP (30 C)\n\nTotal ATP    = 12 + 6 = 18  (3 per CO2)\nTotal NADPH  = 12       (2 per CO2)", "text", title="Table 1. Six turns of the Calvin cycle"),
        T(r"So the cycle needs **3 ATP and 2 NADPH for every CO$_2$ fixed**, and 18 ATP and 12 NADPH for each hexose. Triose phosphate is the key product. It is used to make glucose, then starch (stored in the chloroplast), sucrose (transported in the phloem) and cellulose, and also lipids and, with nitrate, amino acids. Details of how many ATP are used per stage differ slightly between textbooks (some books count the phosphorylation step differently): **check your textbook** and use its numbers if they differ."),
        EX("Worked example 1: atom and energy bookkeeping", intro=r"How many ATP and NADPH are used to make one triose phosphate that leaves the cycle? (Three CO$_2$ are fixed per triose phosphate formed.)", steps=[
            S("CO2 needed", math=r"3\ \mathrm{CO_2}\ \text{for one 3-carbon triose phosphate}"),
            S("ATP", math=r"3 \times 3 = 9\ \text{ATP}"),
            S("NADPH", math=r"3 \times 2 = 6\ \text{NADPH}"),
        ], check=r"2 \times 9 = 18\ \text{ATP},\ \ 2 \times 6 = 12\ \text{NADPH for one hexose}"),
        H("Interpreting changes in the cycle"),
        T(r"Rubisco and the other enzymes are in the stroma, so the amounts of GP and RuBP depend on what happens to the supply of ATP, NADPH and CO$_2$."),
        CODE("Change                   GP                       RuBP\nLight removed            rises (cannot be reduced)  falls (not regenerated)\nCO2 removed              falls (no fixation)        rises (not used up)\nLight on again           falls (reduced to TP)      rises (regenerated)", "text", title="Table 2. What happens when conditions change"),
        T(r"Reasoning: in the dark there is no ATP or NADPH, so GP cannot be converted, and RuBP is not regenerated but is still being used to fix CO$_2$. Without CO$_2$, RuBP is not used but GP is still being converted into TP, so GP falls and RuBP builds up."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a light-off experiment", intro=r"A culture of the alga *Chlorella* is illuminated in the presence of CO$_2$, then the light is switched off. Predict and explain the changes in GP and RuBP.", steps=[
            S("Light stage stops", text="ATP and NADPH quickly run out."),
            S("GP", text="GP is still made by fixation but cannot be reduced, so GP accumulates."),
            S("RuBP", text="RuBP is used up in fixation but not regenerated, so its level falls."),
        ]),
        H("Photorespiration"),
        T(r"Rubisco is not perfectly selective. It can also catalyse the reaction of RuBP with oxygen (**oxygenase activity**). This produces one GP and one two-carbon compound, phosphoglycolate. The phosphoglycolate is processed in the peroxisome and mitochondrion, and CO$_2$ is released. This light-dependent uptake of O$_2$ and release of CO$_2$ is **photorespiration**."),
        D(r"**Photorespiration** is the oxygenase reaction of rubisco, followed by the salvage pathway that releases CO$_2$. It uses ATP, makes no ATP and wastes some of the carbon already fixed, so it **reduces the net rate of photosynthesis**.", title="Photorespiration"),
        T(r"It is more important when the ratio of O$_2$ to CO$_2$ at rubisco is high: in bright, hot, dry weather, when the plant closes its stomata to save water. The CO$_2$ in the leaf falls and O$_2$ from photolysis builds up. Higher temperature also favours the oxygenase reaction. Plants that fix CO$_2$ only by the Calvin cycle (C3 plants) lose a noticeable part of their fixed carbon in this way. The next lesson shows how C4 and CAM plants reduce this loss."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing photorespiration with respiration", r"Respiration occurs in all living cells, day and night, and makes ATP in mitochondria. Photorespiration occurs only in the light in photosynthetic cells, uses rubisco, uses ATP and makes none. They are different processes."),
        QUIZ("The enzyme that joins CO$_2$ to RuBP is:", ["ATP synthase", "Rubisco", "Catalase", "NADP reductase"], 1,
             "Rubisco (ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase) fixes CO2 on to RuBP.",
             hint="It is the most abundant enzyme in leaves.",
             feedback=["ATP synthase makes ATP in the thylakoid membrane.", "", "Catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide.", "NADP reductase reduces NADP in the light stage."]),
        QUIZ("How many ATP are needed to make one hexose in the Calvin cycle (using 3 ATP per CO$_2$)?", ["6", "12", "18", "36"], 2,
             "Six CO2 are fixed for one hexose, so 6 x 3 = 18 ATP.",
             hint="How many CO2 molecules are needed for 6 carbon atoms?",
             feedback=["That would be 1 ATP per CO2.", "That would be 2 ATP per CO2.", "", "That would be 6 ATP per CO2."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"Describe the role of ATP and NADPH in the Calvin cycle.", r"ATP supplies energy to phosphorylate GP (reduction stage) and to regenerate RuBP. NADPH supplies hydrogen (reducing power) to reduce GP to triose phosphate."),
        PQ(r"After CO$_2$ is withdrawn from a lit chloroplast suspension, explain the changes in the concentrations of GP and RuBP.", r"RuBP is no longer used for fixation but is still regenerated from triose phosphate, so its level rises. GP is no longer made but is still converted to triose phosphate, so its level falls."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the number of NADPH needed to make two molecules of triose phosphate that leave the cycle.", r"Each triose phosphate leaving the cycle needs 3 CO2 fixed, and each CO2 needs 2 NADPH. So one triose phosphate needs 6 NADPH, and two need 12 NADPH."),
        PQ(r"Explain why photorespiration is more serious in C3 plants on a hot, dry afternoon.", r"The stomata close to reduce water loss, so CO2 inside the leaf falls while O2 from photolysis rises. A high O2 to CO2 ratio (and a high temperature) favours the oxygenase reaction of rubisco, so more carbon is lost as CO2 from photorespiration and less is fixed."),
        SUM(
            "Fixation: CO2 + RuBP (5C) gives 2 GP (3C) with rubisco. Reduction: GP to triose phosphate with ATP and NADPH. Regeneration: RuBP remade using ATP.",
            "Per CO2 fixed: 3 ATP and 2 NADPH; per hexose: 6 turns, 18 ATP and 12 NADPH.",
            "Triose phosphate is the starting point for glucose, starch, sucrose, cellulose, lipids and amino acids.",
            "No light: GP rises, RuBP falls. No CO2: GP falls, RuBP rises.",
            "Photorespiration: rubisco reacts with O2, wasting fixed carbon, made worse by heat and closed stomata.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 11. C3, C4 and CAM
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=NUT,
    topic="C3, C4 and CAM plants",
    title="C3, C4 and CAM plants",
    slug="bil2-c3-c4-cam-plants",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Compare C3, C4 and CAM carbon fixation, link Kranz leaf anatomy to the C4 pathway, and explain why C4 and CAM plants do well in hot or dry habitats.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the C4 pathway and the Kranz anatomy of a C4 leaf",
        "Explain how C4 plants reduce photorespiration",
        "Describe how CAM plants separate fixation from the Calvin cycle in time",
        "Compare C3, C4 and CAM plants in structure, physiology and habitat",
    ],
    key_concepts=["C3 plant", "C4 plant", "CAM plant", "PEP carboxylase", "Kranz anatomy", "Bundle sheath cell", "Malate", "Stomata at night"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Maize, sugarcane, sorghum and millet are all grown across Cameroon, from the humid South to the dry North. They grow fast in hot sun. Rice, wheat, cassava, cocoa and most trees do not respond in the same way. The difference is partly in how they fix carbon dioxide."),
        H("C3 plants"),
        T(r"In a **C3 plant** CO$_2$ is fixed directly by rubisco in the Calvin cycle in mesophyll chloroplasts, and the first product is the three-carbon compound GP. This is the usual pathway. Its weakness is that rubisco also reacts with oxygen (photorespiration), which is a problem when it is hot, bright and the stomata are partly closed."),
        H("C4 plants and Kranz anatomy"),
        D(r"**C4 plants** first fix CO$_2$ into a four-carbon acid in the mesophyll cells, then release it in the bundle sheath cells, where it is fixed by rubisco in the Calvin cycle. This **concentrates CO$_2$ at rubisco** and cuts photorespiration.", title="C4 plants", note="Examples: maize, sugarcane, sorghum and many tropical grasses."),
        IMG("al-bil2-c4-leaf.svg", alt="C4 leaf section: epidermis with a stoma; mesophyll cells around a ring of large bundle sheath cells enclosing a vascular bundle. Arrows show CO2 entering a mesophyll cell, a four-carbon acid moving into the sheath, and CO2 released there for the Calvin cycle.", caption="Figure 1. Kranz anatomy and the C4 pathway. Mesophyll cells carry out fixation by PEP carboxylase; bundle sheath cells carry out the Calvin cycle."),
        KEY("Kranz anatomy", "Kranz is the German word for wreath.", cards=[
            ["Bundle sheath", "A ring of large cells with thick walls and many chloroplasts around each vascular bundle. They hold rubisco and carry out the Calvin cycle."],
            ["Mesophyll", "Cells outside the ring, close to the stomata. They have PEP carboxylase and fix CO2 first. They have few or no Calvin cycle enzymes."],
            ["Plasmodesmata", "Many channels link the two cell types so that the four-carbon acid can move into the sheath cells."],
            ["Close to the vein", "Sugars made in the sheath cells reach the phloem quickly."],
        ]),
        CODE("In mesophyll cell (cytoplasm):\n  CO2 dissolves to HCO3-; PEP (3C) + HCO3- -> oxaloacetate (4C)  [PEP carboxylase]\n  oxaloacetate -> malate (4C)\nmalate passes through plasmodesmata\nIn bundle sheath cell (chloroplast):\n  malate (4C) -> pyruvate (3C) + CO2     (CO2 concentration rises)\n  CO2 + RuBP -> 2 GP ... Calvin cycle (rubisco)\npyruvate returns to mesophyll:\n  pyruvate + ATP -> PEP     (regenerates PEP; costs extra ATP)", "text", title="The C4 pathway in outline (maize type)"),
        T(r"**PEP carboxylase** has no affinity for oxygen and has a high affinity for the bicarbonate ion, so it fixes CO$_2$ even when the stomata are partly closed and the concentration inside the leaf is low. The CO$_2$ concentration in the bundle sheath becomes high. Rubisco there meets little oxygen, so photorespiration is small. The cost is energy: about two more ATP are used to regenerate PEP for each CO$_2$ fixed, so C4 plants use about five ATP per CO$_2$ instead of three. Details of the type of four-carbon acid (malate or aspartate) vary between species: **check your textbook**."),
        CODE("Feature                      C3 plant             C4 plant\nFirst product of fixation    GP (3C)              oxaloacetate / malate (4C)\nFixing enzyme                rubisco              PEP carboxylase, then rubisco\nWhere Calvin cycle runs      mesophyll            bundle sheath only\nPhotorespiration             significant          very low\nOptimum temperature          lower                higher\nCO2 compensation point       higher               very low\nATP per CO2                  3                    about 5\nExamples                     rice, wheat, cassava maize, sugarcane, sorghum", "text", title="Table 1. C3 and C4 plants"),
        H("CAM plants"),
        D(r"**CAM** stands for crassulacean acid metabolism. In **CAM plants** the stomata open at **night** and close in the day. At night CO$_2$ is fixed by PEP carboxylase into malic acid, which is stored in the vacuole. In the day, with the stomata closed, the acid releases CO$_2$ inside the same cells for the Calvin cycle.", title="CAM plants", note="Examples: pineapple, Kalanchoe, cacti, Agave."),
        T(r"C4 plants separate the two steps in **space** (different cell types). CAM plants separate them in **time** (night and day). Because the stomata open only when it is cool and humid at night, water loss is small. The plant tissue is more acidic at dawn and less acidic at dusk. Growth is slow because the vacuole can store only a limited amount of acid, but the plants survive in very dry places. The pineapple, a CAM plant, is grown in several regions of Cameroon."),
        CODE("Feature            C3                C4                     CAM\nStomata open       day               day (can partly close) night only\nFirst fixation     rubisco           PEP carboxylase        PEP carboxylase (night)\nSeparation of steps none            space (two cell types) time (night/day)\nWater use          high              medium                 very low\nHabitat            temperate/moist   hot, bright, tropical  deserts, dry sites, epiphytes\nExamples           rice, wheat       maize, sugarcane       pineapple, cactus", "text", title="Table 2. C3, C4 and CAM"),
        EX("Worked example 1: why maize does well in hot weather", intro=r"Explain why a maize crop keeps a high rate of photosynthesis on a hot afternoon, when a bean crop does not.", steps=[
            S("The problem for C3", text="Heat and partly closed stomata make photorespiration rise in bean (C3): rubisco wastes carbon."),
            S("Maize concentrates CO2", text="PEP carboxylase fixes CO2 in mesophyll cells even at low internal CO2. Malate carries CO2 to the bundle sheath, where its concentration is high."),
            S("Outcome", text="Rubisco in the sheath cells meets little oxygen, so photorespiration is small and the net rate of photosynthesis stays high."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: reading a leaf section", intro=r"A leaf section shows a ring of large cells with many chloroplasts around each vein, and the mesophyll cells outside the ring contain few starch grains. What type of plant is it, and where is rubisco?", steps=[
            S("Identify the structure", text="A ring of thick-walled bundle sheath cells with many chloroplasts is Kranz anatomy."),
            S("Type", text="It is a C4 plant."),
            S("Rubisco", text="Rubisco is in the bundle sheath chloroplasts; PEP carboxylase is in the mesophyll cells."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: CAM acidity", intro=r"The tissue of a pineapple plant is tested for acidity at 06:00 and at 18:00. Which sample is more acidic, and why?", steps=[
            S("Night", text="At night the stomata are open, CO2 is fixed into malic acid and stored in the vacuole."),
            S("06:00", text="After a night of fixation the acid has built up: more acidic at 06:00."),
            S("18:00", text="During the day the acid has been used up to release CO2: less acidic at 18:00."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying C4 plants do not use the Calvin cycle", r"C4 plants still use the Calvin cycle and rubisco. The extra steps only deliver CO$_2$ to the rubisco in the bundle sheath. Similarly CAM plants use the Calvin cycle in the day."),
        QUIZ("In a C4 plant, which enzyme first fixes CO$_2$ in the mesophyll cells?", ["Rubisco", "PEP carboxylase", "ATP synthase", "Catalase"], 1,
             "PEP carboxylase combines HCO3- with PEP to make a four-carbon acid.",
             hint="It is not the enzyme of the Calvin cycle.",
             feedback=["Rubisco works in the bundle sheath cells.", "", "ATP synthase makes ATP.", "Catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide."]),
        QUIZ("Why can a CAM plant lose less water than a C3 plant?", ["It has no stomata", "It opens its stomata at night", "It has no Calvin cycle", "It has thicker roots"], 1,
             "Stomata open when it is cool, so water vapour loss is less.",
             hint="When is it coolest and most humid?",
             feedback=["CAM plants have stomata.", "", "CAM plants use the Calvin cycle.", "Root thickness is not the cause."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"Describe **three** features of the leaf anatomy of a C4 plant.", r"Bundle sheath cells form a ring (wreath) around each vascular bundle; they are large, with thick walls and many chloroplasts that contain rubisco. Mesophyll cells surround the sheath and contain PEP carboxylase. Many plasmodesmata join the two cell types."),
        PQ(r"Explain how C4 plants have a lower rate of photorespiration than C3 plants.", r"PEP carboxylase fixes CO2 in the mesophyll and malate delivers it to the bundle sheath, where CO2 concentration is high. Rubisco there is in a high CO2 and low O2 environment, so it acts as carboxylase and rarely as oxygenase."),
        PQ(r"State **two** differences between C4 and CAM plants in how they avoid wasteful photorespiration.", r"C4 plants separate fixation and the Calvin cycle in space (mesophyll and bundle sheath), CAM plants separate them in time (night and day). C4 stomata open in the day while CAM stomata open at night. CAM plants store malic acid in the vacuole at night."),
        PQ(r"Suggest why C4 crops usually give higher yields in the tropics than C3 crops in the same field conditions.", r"Hot, bright conditions cause photorespiration in C3 crops but little in C4. C4 plants have a lower CO2 compensation point and keep photosynthesising when stomata are partly closed, so they lose less carbon and water per unit of sugar made and grow faster."),
        SUM(
            "C3: rubisco fixes CO2 directly (first product GP, 3C); photorespiration is a problem in heat.",
            "C4: PEP carboxylase fixes CO2 into a 4C acid in mesophyll; the acid is decarboxylated in bundle sheath cells (Kranz anatomy) where rubisco works at high CO2.",
            "C4 plants lose little carbon to photorespiration, but use more ATP per CO2 (about 5 against 3).",
            "CAM: stomata open at night, CO2 stored as malic acid, released by day for the Calvin cycle: separation in time.",
            "Maize and sugarcane are C4; pineapple and cacti are CAM; rice, wheat and cassava are C3.",
        ),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 12. Digestive histology and control of secretion
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=NUT,
    topic="Digestive histology and control of secretion",
    title="Gut wall histology and the control of digestive secretion",
    slug="bil2-gut-histology-secretion-control",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Describe the layers of the mammalian gut wall and how the stomach and small intestine are adapted, and explain how nerves and the hormones gastrin, secretin and CCK control digestive secretion.",
    objectives=[
        "Name the four layers of the gut wall and state their functions",
        "Relate the structure of the stomach and the small intestine to their functions",
        "Describe the nervous and hormonal control of gastric, pancreatic and bile secretion",
        "Explain how enzyme precursors and mucus protect the gut and pancreas",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Mucosa", "Submucosa", "Muscularis externa", "Serosa", "Villus", "Parietal cell", "Gastrin", "Secretin", "Cholecystokinin (CCK)", "Zymogen"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"After a meal of fufu and eru, the food passes along about nine metres of gut. At each stage a different set of cells and glands adds the right juice at the right time. This lesson looks at the tissues of the gut wall, then at how the body decides when and how much to secrete."),
        H("The four layers of the gut wall"),
        IMG("al-bil2-gut-wall.svg", alt="Gut wall section, lumen outwards: mucosa with epithelium, goblet cells and a villus with capillaries and a lacteal; submucosa with vessels and nerve plexus; muscularis externa with circular and longitudinal muscle; serosa.", caption="Figure 1. Layers of the small intestine wall (schematic). In each villus the orange line is the lacteal and the blue dots are blood capillaries."),
        KEY("From the lumen outwards", "The same four layers are found all along the gut, with local changes.", cards=[
            ["Mucosa (innermost)", "Epithelium (secretes mucus, enzymes; absorbs), a thin layer of connective tissue with small blood vessels and lymphoid tissue, and a thin muscle layer. In the small intestine it is folded into villi."],
            ["Submucosa", "Loose connective tissue with larger blood and lymph vessels and a network of nerves (submucosal plexus). In the duodenum it contains glands that secrete alkaline mucus."],
            ["Muscularis externa", "An inner layer of circular muscle and an outer layer of longitudinal muscle. Their alternating contraction moves food along by peristalsis. A nerve network (myenteric plexus) between the layers coordinates them."],
            ["Serosa (outermost)", "A thin connective tissue layer covered by a smooth membrane that reduces friction with the other organs."],
        ]),
        TIP(r"Do not confuse the muscle layers. The inner muscle is circular, the outer is longitudinal. Circular muscle narrows the gut; longitudinal muscle shortens it."),
        H("The stomach"),
        T(r"The stomach wall has an extra oblique layer of muscle that churns food. The mucosa is folded into pits that lead to glands. Different cells in the glands do different jobs."),
        CODE("Cell                        Product                      Function\nMucous (goblet/neck) cells  mucus and bicarbonate        coats the lining; protects it from acid and pepsin\nChief (zymogenic) cells     pepsinogen (inactive)        converted to pepsin by acid; digests protein\nParietal (oxyntic) cells    hydrochloric acid, intrinsic factor  pH about 2: kills microbes, activates pepsin; intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 absorption\nG cells                     gastrin (a hormone)          stimulates acid and enzyme secretion", "text", title="Table 1. Cells of the stomach glands"),
        T(r"Pepsinogen is stored as a **zymogen**: an inactive enzyme precursor. If active pepsin were stored in the cell it would digest the cell itself. Hydrochloric acid in the stomach lumen changes pepsinogen to pepsin by removing a short chain of amino acids, and pepsin then activates more pepsinogen."),
        H("The small intestine"),
        T(r"The small intestine is where most digestion and absorption occur. Its wall is adapted by the finger-like **villi** (about 1 mm long), whose epithelial cells have **microvilli** (the brush border). The folding and microvilli give a very large surface area. Each villus has a network of blood capillaries to carry away amino acids and sugars, and a central lymph vessel called a **lacteal** to carry fats. **Goblet cells** secrete mucus, and the pits between villi, the **crypts of Lieberkuhn**, contain dividing cells that replace the epithelium every few days. The brush border has enzymes such as maltase and peptidases bound to the membrane."),
        H("Control of secretion"),
        T(r"Secretion is controlled by the **nervous system** (fast, short-lived) and by **hormones** released into the blood by cells in the gut wall (slower, longer lasting). Control is in three phases."),
        D(r"**Cephalic phase**: the sight, smell or taste of food (or thinking of it) stimulates the vagus nerve, which signals the stomach to secrete gastric juice and gastrin before food arrives.", title="1. Cephalic phase"),
        D(r"**Gastric phase**: food stretches the stomach, and peptides and amino acids in it stimulate the G cells to release the hormone **gastrin** into the blood. Gastrin travels round the body and returns to the stomach, where it stimulates the parietal cells to secrete acid and the chief cells to secrete pepsinogen. When the pH falls to about 2, gastrin secretion is inhibited: **negative feedback**.", title="2. Gastric phase"),
        D(r"**Intestinal phase**: acid chyme entering the duodenum stimulates cells in its wall to release the hormone **secretin**, and fats and partly digested protein stimulate release of **cholecystokinin (CCK)**.", title="3. Intestinal phase"),
        CODE("Hormone          Made by                    Stimulus                       Target and effect\nGastrin          G cells of stomach          peptides, stretch, vagus       parietal and chief cells: acid and pepsinogen\nSecretin         duodenum wall (S cells)     acid chyme (low pH)   pancreas: watery fluid rich in hydrogencarbonate;\n                                                                               liver: more bile secretion\nCCK              duodenum wall (I cells)     fats and protein products      pancreas: enzyme-rich juice; gall bladder contracts\n                                                                               to release bile; slows stomach emptying", "text", title="Table 2. Digestive hormones"),
        T(r"Secretin neutralises acid so that the pancreatic enzymes can work at their optimum pH (slightly alkaline). CCK produces the enzymes (amylase, lipase and the protease precursors) and the bile that emulsifies fats. Pancreatic proteases are also made as zymogens: **trypsinogen** is activated to **trypsin** by an enzyme in the intestinal wall (enteropeptidase), and trypsin activates the other zymogens. This protects the pancreas from digesting itself. Textbooks give slightly different pH values and cell names for these hormones: **check your textbook**."),
        EX("Worked example 1: why does the stomach not digest itself?", intro=r"Give **three** ways in which the wall of the stomach is protected from pepsin and acid.", steps=[
            S("Zymogen", text="Pepsin is made as inactive pepsinogen and is only activated in the lumen by acid."),
            S("Mucus", text="Mucous cells secrete a thick layer of mucus and hydrogencarbonate that keeps acid and enzyme away from the epithelium."),
            S("Replacement", text="Epithelial cells are replaced quickly by cell division, so damage is repaired."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: effect of removing a hormone", intro=r"A patient lacks secretin. Predict the effect on digestion in the duodenum.", steps=[
            S("Without secretin", text="The pancreas does not secrete enough hydrogencarbonate-rich fluid."),
            S("Effect on pH", text="The acid chyme is not neutralised, so the duodenal pH stays low."),
            S("Effect on enzymes", text="Pancreatic enzymes have an alkaline optimum and work poorly, so digestion of starch, protein and fat is reduced."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: nervous or hormonal?", intro=r"Explain why the gastric juice begins to flow when you smell food but continues for a long time after you start eating.", steps=[
            S("At the smell", text="Nerves (vagus) respond fast, so secretion starts at once."),
            S("After food reaches the stomach", text="Stretch and peptides cause release of gastrin, which stimulates secretion as long as food is present."),
            S("Comparison", text="Nervous responses are fast and short; hormonal responses are slower but last longer."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Putting the hormones in the wrong place", r"Gastrin is made in the stomach and acts on the stomach. Secretin and CCK are made in the duodenum and act mainly on the pancreas, liver and gall bladder. Secretin gives water and hydrogencarbonate; CCK gives enzymes and bile release."),
        QUIZ("Which cells of the stomach secrete hydrochloric acid?", ["Chief cells", "Parietal cells", "Goblet cells", "G cells"], 1,
             "Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor.",
             hint="Chief cells make pepsinogen.",
             feedback=["Chief cells secrete pepsinogen.", "", "Goblet cells secrete mucus.", "G cells secrete the hormone gastrin."]),
        QUIZ("Which hormone makes the gall bladder contract and the pancreas release enzymes?", ["Gastrin", "Secretin", "CCK", "Insulin"], 2,
             "CCK (cholecystokinin) is released by the duodenum when fats and proteins arrive.",
             hint="Its name means 'gall bladder mover'.",
             feedback=["Gastrin acts on the stomach.", "Secretin increases hydrogencarbonate secretion from the pancreas.", "", "Insulin is made by the pancreas in the islets and controls blood glucose."]),
        H("Practice"),
        PQ(r"Describe how the structure of the wall of the small intestine is adapted to absorb digested food.", r"The mucosa is folded into villi and the epithelial cells have microvilli, giving a large surface area. The epithelium is one cell thick, a short diffusion path. Each villus has a dense capillary network and a lacteal to carry away the products, which keeps the concentration gradient steep. Many mitochondria in the epithelial cells supply ATP for active transport."),
        PQ(r"Explain the part played by negative feedback in the control of gastric acid secretion.", r"Gastrin stimulates parietal cells to release acid. As the pH of the stomach contents falls to about 2, the secretion of gastrin is inhibited, so less acid is made. When food raises the pH again, gastrin release increases. This keeps the pH within a narrow range."),
        PQ(r"Describe the roles of secretin and CCK when acid chyme enters the duodenum.", r"Secretin is released in response to acid and causes the pancreas to release a watery, hydrogencarbonate-rich juice that neutralises the chyme (and stimulates bile secretion). CCK is released in response to fats and proteins and causes the pancreas to secrete enzymes and the gall bladder to contract, releasing bile."),
        PQ(r"Suggest why the muscle layers of the gut wall are arranged in two directions.", r"The circular muscle narrows the lumen and the longitudinal muscle shortens it. Alternating contraction and relaxation produce peristalsis, which pushes food along, and also mix food with enzymes."),
        SUM(
            "Gut wall layers from the lumen: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa (circular inside, longitudinal outside) and serosa.",
            "Stomach: mucous cells (mucus), chief cells (pepsinogen), parietal cells (HCl), G cells (gastrin). Small intestine: villi, microvilli, goblet cells, lacteals.",
            "Nervous control is fast; hormonal control (gastrin, secretin, CCK) is slower and longer lasting; acid in the stomach inhibits gastrin by negative feedback.",
            "Secretin: hydrogencarbonate-rich pancreatic fluid; CCK: pancreatic enzymes and gall bladder contraction.",
            "Zymogens (pepsinogen, trypsinogen) and mucus prevent self-digestion.",
        ),
    ],
))
